/* Bản dịch kịch bản «Giải mã tác phẩm» — English (en). Claude dịch từ data/works.js, kiểm tra bằng i18n/validate.js (cập nhật 30/10/2026: 30 tác phẩm).
   {chữ hiện|cách đọc}: người xem thấy phần trước, giọng AI đọc phần sau (số, năm, thế kỷ, chữ khó đọc). */
window.ART_I18N = window.ART_I18N || {};
window.ART_I18N.en = {"lang":"en","v":2,"works":{"dong-ho-dam-cuoi-chuot":{"title":"The Mouse Wedding","artist":"Artisans of Đông Hồ village (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design (Đông Hồ printmaking dates from around the 17th century)","medium":"Woodblock print on dó paper coated with điệp (powdered scallop shell), natural pigments","where":"Đông Hồ painting village (Thuận Thành ward, Bắc Ninh); prints held in many museums","place":{"name":"Đông Hồ painting village","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["Đông Hồ","Lunar New Year","satire","woodblock print","Vietnamese folk art"],"hook":"At first glance, it's just a cheerful mouse wedding, the kind of picture people put up for Tết, the Lunar New Year. But look closely, and you'll find a bribe being paid in plain sight, in broad daylight.","intro":"The Mouse Wedding, a woodblock print from Đông Hồ village, Bắc Ninh. Each color has its own block, printed on handmade dó paper coated with crushed seashell.","openers":["This is a wedding for the mouse family, so why is the biggest figure in the picture a cat?","There's a tiny detail next to the mouse offering a bird to the cat: two Chinese characters that read Tống lễ.","Try to find the two mice blowing horns. That music can be read in two completely opposite ways."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a painting of mice…","end":"A mouse picture made for New Year laughs, yet it says so much about power and palm-greasing in an entire society. Now you understand why people can spend hours looking at The Mouse Wedding.","img":[{"by":"Đông Hồ artisans · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🐱 Cat = the powerful","q":"Did you notice who's the biggest figure in the picture?","a":"Not the bride, not the groom, but the cat. It takes up the whole top corner, one paw raised as if giving orders. According to the Đông Hồ artisans, the cat stands for those in power, and the mice are the common people.","s":"Not the bride, but the cat. According to the artisans, the cat stands for the powerful, and the mice for ordinary people."},{"tag":"🐦 Tống lễ = giving gifts","q":"What is the lead mouse offering to the cat?","a":"A bird. Right below it are two Chinese characters, Tống lễ, meaning 'delivering gifts'. If the wedding is to go off in peace, the mice have to grease the cat's palm first.","s":"A bird, labeled Tống lễ, 'delivering gifts'. For a peaceful wedding, the cat has to be paid off first."},{"tag":"🐟 Fish and bird","q":"And what's the second mouse holding?","a":"A carp. Birds and fish are a cat's favorite treats. Look at how the gift-bearing mice bow and scrape: that's the posture of someone who's afraid."},{"tag":"🎺 Tác nhạc = strike up","q":"Are those two horn-blowing mice celebrating the wedding, or up to something else?","a":"Nearby are two characters, Tác nhạc, meaning 'strike up the music'. Some say the horns are there to keep the cat happy while it accepts its gifts. Others read it the opposite way: the horns blare so the whole village hears about the bribe."},{"tag":"📜 Four Chinese characters","q":"What do the four Chinese characters at top left say?","a":"The press usually reads them as Lão thử thủ thân, but there's still no agreement on how to read this line or what it means. The mouse wedding is also a theme in Chinese New Year prints, where one version ends with the cat eating the bride. In Đông Hồ, the craftsmen kept the gift-giving scene and turned it into satire."},{"tag":"🐴 The groom on horseback","q":"So where's the groom?","a":"According to artisan Nguyễn Hữu Sam, here he is: riding a rosy-red horse, wearing a hat, dressed like a mandarin, with a parasol held up behind him. Some sources read the characters beside him as Chủ hôn, the person hosting the wedding. Whoever he is, this mouse wedding is staged exactly like a wedding of the elite.","s":"On a rosy-red horse, in a hat, dressed like a mandarin (some read him as the wedding host): the mouse wedding copies a wedding of the elite."},{"tag":"👰 The bridal palanquin","q":"Where's the bride sitting?","a":"In a yellow-roofed palanquin carried by four mice. There are signboards, parasols and music, everything a proper bridal procession needs. And it's that very pomp that makes the bribery scene above all the more ironic.","s":"In a yellow-roofed palanquin carried by four mice, as grand as any proper bridal procession."},{"tag":"〰️ Two-tier storytelling","q":"Why is there a wavy line splitting the picture in two?","a":"Folk paintings don't use Western-style perspective. That wavy line separates two scenes: on top, the procession bringing gifts to the cat; below, the procession escorting the bride. The two stories follow each other like two panels of a comic strip."},{"tag":"❔ Five black dots","q":"And what about the five little black dots on the left?","a":"The honest answer: no one knows for sure. For dots like these on mouse paintings, artisan Nguyễn Hữu Sam reads them as the five elements, a wish for the mouse family to thrive in harmony. That's one reading, not the only answer."},{"tag":"🎨 Colors from nature","q":"Where do these colors come from?","a":"All from nature: red from red-earth pebbles, yellow from pagoda-tree flowers, blue from indigo leaves, black from the ash of bamboo leaves or sticky-rice straw. The shimmering background is powdered scallop shell in sticky-rice paste. Each color has its own block; the black outlines go on last.","s":"Red from red-earth pebbles, yellow from pagoda-tree flowers, blue from indigo, black from bamboo-leaf ash. The shimmer is powdered scallop shell."}]},"dong-ho-lon-nai":{"title":"Herd of Yin-Yang Pigs","artist":"Artisans of Đông Hồ village (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design","medium":"Woodblock print on dó paper coated with điệp (powdered scallop shell), natural pigments","where":"Đông Hồ painting village (Thuận Thành ward, Bắc Ninh)","place":{"name":"Đông Hồ painting village","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["Đông Hồ","blessing print","yin and yang","Vietnamese folk art"],"hook":"A big, round mother pig and her piglets, pasted up for Tết, the Lunar New Year. But look at the swirl on her body: it's a whole universe in miniature.","intro":"Herd of Yin-Yang Pigs, a woodblock print from Đông Hồ village. There isn't a single word on it; the picture itself speaks the blessing.","openers":["Why, in many Đông Hồ pig prints, does the mother pig have exactly five piglets, each a different color?","One detail on this pig's body comes straight from how farmers of old picked their breeding stock.","Stop and look at the mother pig's snout: is it in profile, or is it looking straight at you?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a painting of pigs…","end":"Not a single word, just swirls and a herd of plump pigs, yet people long ago packed into it both the philosophy of yin and yang and the dream of plenty. Now you understand why people can look at it for hours.","img":[{"by":"Đông Hồ artisans · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"☯️ Hair whorl = yin-yang","q":"What's that swirl on the mother pig's back?","a":"It's the yin-yang symbol. But it comes from something very down-to-earth: the whorl in a pig's bristles. Farmers of old saw a whorl as the mark of good breeding stock, and the printmakers turned it into a yin-yang circle, a wish for the herd to keep multiplying without end.","s":"A yin-yang symbol, born from a hair whorl, the mark of a good breeder: a wish for endless offspring."},{"tag":"🐷 The Ỉ pig breed","q":"What breed is this pig, to be so round and fat?","a":"It's the Ỉ pig, an old breed from northern Vietnam, with a sagging back and a big belly. Real pigs back then were much skinnier. The picture doesn't show what people had, but what farmers hoped for: a full, well-fed year.","s":"The Ỉ pig, an old northern breed. The picture shows not what people had, but what they wished for: a year of plenty."},{"tag":"👃 Side and front at once","q":"Is this pig's snout shown from the side or from the front?","a":"Both. Writer Phùng Hồng Kổn points out that the snout is drawn in profile and head-on at the same time. Folk artists didn't need the rules of perspective; they drew things the way they'd be seen most clearly."},{"tag":"5️⃣ Five piglets, five hues","q":"How many piglets does the mother have?","a":"In this version, five piglets, each a different color. Artisan Nguyễn Hữu Quả says five is just right for the five elements. That's how the villagers read the picture; the number of piglets can vary from one woodblock to another.","s":"Five, each a different color. Đông Hồ artisans read that as exactly the five elements."},{"tag":"🐖 Kids by the dozen","q":"What's the piglet on the right up to?","a":"Climbing onto its mother, head up as if laughing. Each piglet strikes its own pose: one climbs, one snuggles up to mom, one is busy eating. It's a picture of children and grandchildren by the dozen, the big, lively household people wished for every New Year."},{"tag":"☯️ From mother to piglets","q":"Did you notice the piglets have swirls too?","a":"That's right. The woodblocks have been recarved over many generations, and details may change, but the yin-yang swirl is always kept, like a blessing passed from mother to child."},{"tag":"🌙 Volume from color","q":"What are those crescent shapes on the pig's body for?","a":"That's how the artisans create volume. No cast shadows, no light and shade, just a few curved patches of color, and the pig's body looks round and full."},{"tag":"🌱 Something to eat","q":"And the little plant at the pigs' feet?","a":"Probably food: one piglet is munching on the leaves. For farmers, a herd of well-fed pigs is itself a wish for a prosperous year."},{"tag":"✨ Seashell paper","q":"Why does the background shimmer?","a":"That's the điệp coating: scallop shells ground fine, mixed with sticky-rice paste and brushed onto handmade dó paper. Under the light, the pigs seem to glow. The poet Hoàng Cầm mentioned chicken and pig paintings on shell-coated paper in his poem 'Across the Đuống River'.","s":"The điệp coating: powdered scallop shell and sticky-rice paste brushed onto dó paper, which makes the pigs seem to glow."}]},"dong-ho-ga":{"title":"Đông Hồ Rooster Painting","artist":"Artisans of Đông Hồ village (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design (the custom of hanging rooster paintings dates back to the 16th century)","medium":"Woodblock print on dó paper, natural pigments","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, Hanoi (the rooster-and-hen version shown)","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Đông Hồ","Lunar New Year","five virtues","woodblock print"],"hook":"At Tết, the Lunar New Year, people used to paste rooster paintings on their doors. But that rooster wasn't just decoration: it carries five virtues the ancestors wanted the young to learn.","intro":"A Đông Hồ rooster painting, a woodblock print on handmade dó paper. The version you're seeing has a rooster, a hen and their chicks, and it's now in the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["That red comb on the rooster's head: whose hat did people of old compare it to?","There's one virtue of the rooster you can't see in this picture, no matter how closely you look.","Try to find the spur on the rooster's leg. To people of old, it stood for an entire virtue."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a painting of a rooster…","end":"Learning, martial skill, courage, benevolence and trust: five virtues wrapped up in one rooster on dó paper. Now you understand why rooster paintings went up right on the front door every spring.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Đông Hồ artisans · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🎓 Comb = Learning","q":"What does the red comb on the rooster's head look like?","a":"People of old compared it to a scholar's hat. So the comb stands for Văn, learning, the first of the rooster's five virtues."},{"tag":"⚔️ Spurs = Martial skill","q":"What virtue are these legs hiding?","a":"A rooster's legs have spurs, weapons made for fighting. The spurs stand for Võ, martial skill."},{"tag":"🦁 Chest out = Courage","q":"And that puffed-out chest?","a":"That's Dũng, courage: meet a rival, charge right in, never back down."},{"tag":"⏰ Crowing on time = Trust","q":"What do the Chinese characters on this rooster painting say?","a":"Dạ xướng ngũ canh hòa: crowing through the night, steady through all five night watches. Back before clocks, the rooster's crow marked each watch. Crowing right on time: that's Tín, trustworthiness."},{"tag":"❤️ Sharing = Benevolence","q":"And the fifth virtue, the one you can't actually see?","a":"It's Nhân, benevolence: when the rooster finds food, he calls the whole flock over to share it. A picture can't show that call, but it can show the result: the hen and her chicks gathered close together."},{"tag":"🐣 The chicks","q":"Can you spot the chick climbing onto its mother's back?","a":"Each chick has its own pose: one climbs on mom's back, one grabs for food, one flaps its wings. A hen with her brood is a wish for a warm home full of children and grandchildren."},{"tag":"🦵 Golden rooster stance","q":"Why is this rooster standing on just one leg?","a":"That's the kim kê độc lập pose, 'the golden rooster stands on one leg': the steady, proud stance of a rooster as it lets out its crow."},{"tag":"🖨️ One block per color","q":"How was this dazzling tail printed?","a":"Each color has its own woodblock, printed one over another; the black outline block goes last, tying all the patches of color together."},{"tag":"🚪 Guarding the door","q":"How long have Vietnamese people hung rooster paintings at Tết?","a":"Since around the sixteenth or seventeenth century: a poem by Hoàng Sĩ Khải describes rooster paintings hung in the capital, Thăng Long, today's Hanoi, at Tết to ward off evil. The rooster's crow calls up the sun and chases away the dark, just what people want for a new year.","s":"Since around the sixteenth or seventeenth century: the rooster calls up the sun and chases away the dark, just what people want for a new year."}]},"hang-trong-ngu-ho":{"title":"The Five Tigers","artist":"Artisans of Hàng Trống street, Hanoi (anonymous)","date":"Traditional design (flourished in the late 19th – early 20th century)","medium":"Half printed, half painted: outlines printed from a woodblock, colors applied by hand on dó paper","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, Hanoi (the version shown)","place":{"name":"Hàng Trống street, Hoàn Kiếm","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Hàng Trống","devotional painting","five elements","Vietnamese folk art"],"hook":"Five tigers, five colors, among clouds and rocks. But follow them clockwise, and the painting will read you the whole cycle of heaven and earth.","intro":"The Five Tigers, a Hàng Trống painting from Hanoi. Only the outlines are printed from a woodblock; the colors are painted by hand, layer by layer, so no two copies are exactly alike.","openers":["Why, in Mother Goddess temples, do the five Lord Tigers sit at the lowest altar and receive offerings of raw meat?","There's a small detail under the yellow tiger's paws: a command tablet, and the writing on it isn't the same in every version.","Stop and look at both sides of the pack: a sword on one side, a command flag on the other, like an army."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours in front of a painting of five tigers…","end":"Five tigers, five colors, five directions, arranged in an endless cycle of creation. Now you understand why people can stand in front of The Five Tigers for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot · Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🟡 Yellow tiger = Earth","q":"Why is the yellow tiger in the middle so much bigger?","a":"That's Hoàng hổ, the Yellow Tiger, linked to Earth, at the center where the other four elements meet. In temples, the yellow tiger commands the whole pack."},{"tag":"⚪ White tiger = Metal","q":"Who's the white tiger riding the clouds up in the corner?","a":"Bạch hổ, the White Tiger: Metal, the west, autumn. The two tigers riding clouds at the top belong to heaven; the two standing on rocks below belong to earth."},{"tag":"⚫ Black tiger = Water","q":"And the black tiger on the right?","a":"Hắc hổ, the Black Tiger: Water, the north, winter."},{"tag":"🔴 Red tiger = Fire","q":"Who's the red tiger snarling in the bottom corner?","a":"Xích hổ, the Red Tiger: Fire, the south, summer. By convention, the remaining one on the right is Thanh hổ, the Green Tiger: Wood, the east, spring. Because the colors are painted by hand, every copy has its own shades."},{"tag":"🔄 The generating cycle","q":"Now go clockwise, starting from the green tiger. What do you see?","a":"In this common color arrangement: green, then red, then yellow in the middle, then white, then black. That's the generating cycle of the five elements: Wood gives rise to Fire, Fire to Earth, Earth to Metal, Metal to Water, and Water back to Wood. The five tigers form a cycle that never ends.","s":"Green, red, yellow, white, black: the generating cycle of the five elements. Wood gives rise to Fire, Fire to Earth, Earth to Metal, Metal to Water."},{"tag":"✨ A constellation","q":"What are those little connected dots above the yellow tiger's head?","a":"A constellation; some say it's the Big Dipper. The sky, the stars and the five directions, all packed into one frame: The Five Tigers is like a miniature map of the universe."},{"tag":"🗡️ Lords of Five Camps","q":"Why do the tigers come with a sword and a command flag?","a":"Because this is a heavenly army. In Mother Goddess temples, the five Lord Tigers are called quan Ngũ dinh, the officers in charge of five army camps. The sword means authority; the flag means command."},{"tag":"📜 Command tablet","q":"What's the little tablet under the yellow tiger's paws?","a":"A command tablet, a sign of divine authority. The writing on it differs from copy to copy; some show the character Vương, 'king'."},{"tag":"🏮 The lowest altar","q":"Where is this painting worshipped?","a":"In temples and shrines to the Mother Goddess, the five tigers sit at the lowest altar, just below the main one, guarding the temple door. Offerings to the Lord Tigers are usually raw meat and raw eggs. At Tết, the Lunar New Year, many families hang a tiger painting to keep their homes safe.","s":"At the lowest altar in Mother Goddess temples, guarding the door; offerings are usually raw meat and raw eggs."},{"tag":"🖌️ Half print, half paint","q":"Look really close at the tiger's fur. What do you see?","a":"Layers of color blended by hand, shading from dark to light little by little. That's the big difference between Hàng Trống and Đông Hồ: Đông Hồ prints flat colors from woodblocks, while Hàng Trống prints only the outlines and paints the colors in by hand."}]},"to-ngoc-van-thieu-nu-ben-hoa-hue":{"title":"Young Woman with Lilies","artist":"Tô Ngọc Vân (1906–1954)","date":"1943","medium":"Oil","where":"Whereabouts of the original unknown (the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts was still searching for it as of December 2025)","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts (home of its “twin”, Young Woman with Lotus Flowers)","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Indochinese art","oil painting","áo dài","Tô Ngọc Vân"],"hook":"This may well be the most famous Vietnamese painting of all. But here's something few people know: many of the prints out there actually show copies, and the museum is still searching for the original.","intro":"Young Woman with Lilies, an oil painting by Tô Ngọc Vân, {1943|nineteen forty-three}.","openers":["Do you know who the girl in white beside this vase of flowers is?","There's a twist hidden in the painting's Vietnamese name: the flower it names, huệ, isn't the one most Vietnamese would picture.","Try counting how many different shades of white are in this painting."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at one girl beside a vase of lilies…","end":"A quiet moment, a few Easter lilies and so many shades of white. Now you understand why people can look at Young Woman with Lilies for a lifetime and never tire of it.","img":[{"by":"Tô Ngọc Vân · image circulated on Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"👁️ A dreamy gaze","q":"Have you noticed the young woman's eyes?","a":"She isn't looking at us. Her eyes are half-closed, her head tilted toward the vase, as if lost in some very private thought. It's that quiet moment that makes it so hard to look away."},{"tag":"🌸 Huệ tây = Easter lily","q":"So what flower is it, really?","a":"The Vietnamese title says huệ, but this is huệ tây, also called loa kèn: the Easter lily, which blooms pure white in Hanoi around late March and early April. It's nothing like the native huệ, the tuberose people offer with incense. The lily stands for purity.","s":"Huệ tây, the Easter lily of April in Hanoi, not the tuberose offered with incense. It stands for purity."},{"tag":"✋ A closed curve","q":"One hand is raised to her hair. Why?","a":"That gesture closes the composition: her arm, her head and the flowers form a curve that wraps around her face. Your eye travels around it, then comes to rest on her face."},{"tag":"🤍 A gentle touch","q":"And the other hand?","a":"It barely touches a petal. Not holding, not picking. A gesture so light, as if she's afraid of hurting the flower."},{"tag":"🤍 White on white","q":"How many shades of white can you count?","a":"A white áo dài, the traditional long dress, white flowers, a pale vase, yet no two whites are the same. The painter mixed white with creamy yellow, blue and pink into countless shades. Painting white on white and keeping it all distinct: that's the masterful challenge of this work."},{"tag":"🏺 A touch of blue","q":"What's the blue-and-white vase doing here?","a":"In a picture that's all yellow and white, the blue patterns on the vase are a rare accent that pulls your eye toward the flowers."},{"tag":"🧑‍🎨 A model named Sáu","q":"Who was the young woman in the painting?","a":"According to the painter's son, she was a professional model named Sáu, whom Tô Ngọc Vân had hired since {1939|nineteen thirty-nine}. She later sat for Nguyễn Gia Trí and Trần Văn Cẩn as well. By the time the son went looking for her, she had passed away.","s":"According to the painter's son, it was Sáu, a professional model he had hired since {1939|nineteen thirty-nine}."},{"tag":"🔎 Where's the original?","q":"Why isn't one of Vietnam's most famous paintings a National Treasure?","a":"According to one expert, because no one has been able to verify which one is the original. The painting passed through many collectors, the museum once hung a copy, and a book from {1983|nineteen eighty-three} even printed a fake by mistake. In {December 2025|December twenty twenty-five}, the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts said it was still searching for the original.","s":"According to one expert, the original has never been verified: it changed hands many times, and copies and fakes exist. The museum is still searching."},{"tag":"👯 Its twin painting","q":"This painting has a twin sister?","a":"Yes: Young Woman with Lotus Flowers, with the same model, held by the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts. After restoration, it went on public display for the first time in {December 2025|December twenty twenty-five}."}]},"to-ngoc-van-hai-thieu-nu-va-em-be":{"title":"Two Young Women and a Child","artist":"Tô Ngọc Vân (1906–1954)","date":"1944","medium":"Oil on canvas","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, 66 Nguyễn Thái Học, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Indochinese art","oil painting","national treasure","Tô Ngọc Vân"],"hook":"Two young women chatting on a porch, a little boy playing on the floor. A perfectly ordinary scene. But that boy in red is the painter's own son.","intro":"Two Young Women and a Child, oil on canvas, by Tô Ngọc Vân, {1944|nineteen forty-four}. A National Treasure, now in the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Did you know that, according to the painter's family, this painting once had a name tied to a flower?","One color repeats several times in this painting, and it's probably no accident.","Try to work out what shape the three figures in this painting make."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at one quiet afternoon on a porch…","end":"A peaceful family afternoon, preserved in warm yellow and a flower that blooms at dawn and fades by nightfall. Now you understand why people can look at it for hours.","img":[{"by":"Tô Ngọc Vân · photo: Wikimedia Commons","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"👦 The painter's son","q":"Who's the little boy in red in the corner?","a":"According to Tô Ngọc Thành himself, the painter's son, it's him at the age of four. The picture was painted right in the family's home on Khâm Thiên Street."},{"tag":"👩 Mai the teacher","q":"And the young woman in yellow sitting on the bamboo daybed?","a":"Also according to Thành, the model was Mai, a teacher and a friend of the painter's wife. The girl in white sitting on the floor was the painter's niece."},{"tag":"🔺 Triangular composition","q":"Can you see what shape the three figures form?","a":"A triangle: the woman in yellow at the top, the girl in white and the little boy at the two bottom corners. The triangular composition of classical Western painting brings calm and balance to a thoroughly Vietnamese scene."},{"tag":"🌺 The cotton rose","q":"What flower is that in the background?","a":"According to the painter's family, it's phù dung, the cotton rose, a flower that blooms in the morning and fades by night. Tô Ngọc Vân compared a young woman's life to this flower, and the painting's first title was Young Women and Cotton Roses.","s":"According to the painter's family, the cotton rose, which blooms at dawn and fades by night; the painting's first title was Young Women and Cotton Roses."},{"tag":"🌸 Three touches of pink","q":"Have you noticed how many times pink shows up?","a":"The flower in the hand of the woman in yellow, the hair clip of the girl in white, and the flowers behind them. Three touches of pink, as if echoing the flower in the painting's first title. That's one reading; you can check it for yourself."},{"tag":"🤍 The girl in white","q":"What is the girl in white doing?","a":"Sitting on the floor, looking up and listening. Her white áo dài, the traditional long dress, fans out over the tiles, against the warm yellow of the woman seated above."},{"tag":"☀️ Warm yellow tones","q":"Why does the whole painting seem bathed in sunlight?","a":"Warm yellow covers everything: the clothes, the bamboo daybed, the floor, the wall. According to its National Treasure dossier, that yellow binds people and nature into one harmonious whole."},{"tag":"🎋 A Hanoi porch","q":"What does the bamboo blind overhead tell us?","a":"A bamboo blind, a bamboo daybed, a porch: everyday Vietnamese things placed in a composition learned from classical Western painting."},{"tag":"💰 200 đồng (1965)","q":"What price was this National Treasure once sold for?","a":"Around {1965|nineteen sixty-five}, its keeper, the physician Nguyễn Tấn Gi Trọng, a friend of the painter, let the museum have it for {200 đồng|two hundred dong}. And in {1970|nineteen seventy}, Tô Ngọc Thành was surprised to find prints of it being sold as souvenirs in Prague.","s":"Around {1965|nineteen sixty-five}, it went to the museum for {200 đồng|two hundred dong}; in {1970|nineteen seventy}, prints of it were even sold as souvenirs in Prague."}]},"nguyen-phan-chanh-choi-o-an-quan":{"title":"Playing Ô Ăn Quan","artist":"Nguyễn Phan Chánh (1892–1984)","date":"1931","medium":"Silk","where":"Fukuoka Asian Art Museum, Japan","place":{"name":"Fukuoka Asian Art Museum","prov":"Abroad · Japan"},"tags":["silk painting","Indochinese art","folk games","Nguyễn Phan Chánh"],"hook":"Four little girls sit right on the ground, playing a familiar pebble game. But look closely: almost every eye in the picture is fixed on a single hand.","intro":"Playing Ô Ăn Quan, a silk painting by Nguyễn Phan Chánh, {1931|nineteen thirty-one}.","openers":["Can you guess which country this silk painting is kept in today?","One thing about this painting was once dismissed by a French official as not to buyers' taste.","Try to find the game board in this painting. Hint: don't look on a table."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a single painting on silk…","end":"A children's game, a humble country brown, and one hand at the center of it all. Now you understand why Playing Ô Ăn Quan is seen as a milestone of Vietnamese silk painting.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Fukuoka Asian Art Museum","lic":"© Nguyễn Phan Chánh"}],"steps":[{"tag":"✋ The focal point","q":"Did you notice the hand making a move?","a":"Every gaze points to it. The painter said he deliberately had the youngest girl make the first move, so the whole group would focus on a single spot. That hand is the heart of the whole painting."},{"tag":"⚖️ Three against one","q":"Why are the girls sitting three against one?","a":"The painter said he put one girl on one side and three huddled together on the other, so the composition wouldn't feel scattered and the light and dark areas would balance out."},{"tag":"🪨 A board on the ground","q":"So where's the game board?","a":"Drawn right on the ground: small squares hold pebbles, the game pieces, and the two end squares are the 'mandarin' squares. In the old days, ô ăn quan was a game for girls of about seven to ten."},{"tag":"🧣 A winter's day","q":"What does the girl's headscarf tell us about the season?","a":"It's a khăn mỏ quạ, the 'crow's beak' headscarf of northern Vietnamese women. The painter's diary describes the children bundled up on cold days in Kim Liên village. This is a winter's day."},{"tag":"🟤 Deep brown tones","q":"Why is the whole painting steeped in deep brown?","a":"It's the brown of village clothing, with touches of black, yellow and red. In {1932|nineteen thirty-two}, when the painting was sold in Paris, a French official even complained the colors were too dull for French buyers' taste. Yet that subdued palette later became the signature of Nguyễn Phan Chánh's silk paintings."},{"tag":"💧 Paint, then wash","q":"How did he make the silk look as soft as mist?","a":"Nguyễn Phan Chánh would paint a layer of color, let it dry, wash the painting in water, then paint and wash again, many times over. The color soaks so deep into the silk that both sides look almost the same."},{"tag":"✍️ Poem and seal","q":"What are those lines of Chinese characters in the corner?","a":"His silk paintings often carry a poem in Chinese characters, a red seal and a signature. The poem doesn't necessarily describe the scene in the picture."},{"tag":"🗾 Fukuoka, Japan","q":"Where is this painting today?","a":"In Japan, at the Fukuoka Asian Art Museum. In {1932|nineteen thirty-two}, it sold in Paris for two thousand francs and was printed in color in a famous French magazine. The painter also made several other versions of the same subject."}]},"tran-van-can-em-thuy":{"title":"Little Thúy","artist":"Trần Văn Cẩn (1910–1994)","date":"1943","medium":"Oil on canvas","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, 66 Nguyễn Thái Học, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["portrait","oil painting","national treasure","Trần Văn Cẩn"],"hook":"An eight-year-old girl sits in a rattan chair, her hands folded together. Some call this painting the Mona Lisa of Vietnam. Why?","intro":"Little Thúy, oil on canvas, by Trần Văn Cẩn, {1943|nineteen forty-three}. A National Treasure, at the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Did you know the girl in this painting was a real person, and lived until {2024|twenty twenty-four}?","One part of this painting was once the most badly damaged, and you're probably looking straight at it.","Take a close look at the girl's dress: is it really white?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a portrait of one little girl…","end":"A little girl, a pink silk dress, and a gaze that reaches across more than eighty years. Now you understand why people call Little Thúy the Mona Lisa of Vietnam.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"© Trần Văn Cẩn"}],"steps":[{"tag":"👁️ The gaze","q":"Who are those shining black eyes looking at?","a":"Straight out at us, round and shining black. Restorer Caroline Fry called the painting the Mona Lisa of Vietnam, and British composer Paul Zetter felt the girl seemed to follow the viewer with her eyes."},{"tag":"👧 Nguyễn Minh Thúy","q":"Who was Little Thúy?","a":"Her name was Nguyễn Minh Thúy, born in {1935|nineteen thirty-five}, and she called the painter 'uncle'. At eight, she sat for him through many sessions on Hàng Cót Street. She grew up to be a teacher and passed away in {2024|twenty twenty-four}, at the age of eighty-nine."},{"tag":"🎀 Pale pink silk","q":"The dress looks white, but what color is it really?","a":"It's pale pink Hà Đông silk. It was seeing the girl in that very dress that made the painter want to paint her."},{"tag":"🤲 A little shy","q":"What do those folded hands tell us?","a":"A touch of shyness, from an eight-year-old made to sit still in front of an easel. Her body leans slightly forward, her hands resting obediently in her lap."},{"tag":"🪑 The rattan chair","q":"What role does the rattan chair play?","a":"The brown of the rattan wraps around the pale dress and makes her face stand out. Broad areas of color, loose brushwork: restorer Caroline Fry praised that very style for its Eastern spirit."},{"tag":"🩹 Saving the painting","q":"Which part of the painting was once the most badly damaged?","a":"The face itself. By {2003|two thousand three}, the painting was covered in dust, soot and insect droppings, and the paint was blistering and flaking. Caroline Fry, an expert from Australia, worked for months at the museum and handed the painting back on {June 28, 2004|June twenty-eighth, two thousand four}."},{"tag":"🔎 Lost and found","q":"How did the painting go missing?","a":"In {1943|nineteen forty-three}, it won first prize at an exhibition in Hanoi, then went missing during the war. One source says the museum bought it back in {1964|nineteen sixty-four} for {300 đồng|three hundred dong}; another says it was found in a barber's house."},{"tag":"🎻 From painting to music","q":"Can a painting give birth to a piece of music?","a":"Yes. After seeing the painting, composer Paul Zetter wrote a minuet dedicated to Little Thúy, and in {2024|twenty twenty-four}, he gave the rights to the piece to the museum."}]},"trong-dong-ngoc-lu":{"title":"The Ngọc Lũ Bronze Drum","artist":"People of the Đông Sơn culture","date":"About 2,000–2,500 years ago (3rd–2nd century BCE)","medium":"Bronze","where":"National Museum of Vietnamese History, 1 Tràng Tiền, Hanoi","place":{"name":"National Museum of Vietnamese History","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Đông Sơn","bronze drum","national treasure","archaeology"],"hook":"A drum dug up by chance while villagers were building a dike, now at the top of Vietnam's very first list of National Treasures. Its face holds the whole world of the ancient Vietnamese, if you're willing to look really slowly.","intro":"The Ngọc Lũ bronze drum, from the Đông Sơn culture, some two thousand to two and a half thousand years old. The drawing you're seeing recreates the patterns on the face of a Đông Sơn drum.","openers":["Why do the people, deer and birds on this drum all move counterclockwise?","There's a detail right in the center of this drum: a number that still has no agreed explanation.","Try to find a row of drums on this drum's face, with someone pounding down on them with a long pole."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a single bronze drum…","end":"One drum that holds the sun, the harvests, the festivals and the boats of the ancient Vietnamese. Now you understand why the Ngọc Lũ drum is a National Treasure.","img":[{"by":"Drawing of Đông Sơn drum patterns: Tom Mai","lic":"Public domain"},{"by":"Photo: VuThiAnh · National Museum of Vietnamese History","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🥁 Dug up building a dike","q":"What does the real drum look like?","a":"Sixty-three centimeters tall, with a face nearly eighty centimeters across, covered in a gray-green patina. Around {1893|eighteen ninety-three}, villagers building a dike dug it up and took it to be worshipped in the Ngọc Lũ village communal house. In {1903|nineteen oh three}, it was brought to Hanoi.","s":"Around {1893|eighteen ninety-three}, villagers building a dike dug it up and enshrined it in the Ngọc Lũ communal house."},{"tag":"☀️ A 14-ray sun","q":"How many points does the star in the center of the drum have?","a":"On the Ngọc Lũ drum, fourteen. Most researchers see it as the sun, tied to the sun worship of rice-growing peoples. Why fourteen? No one has been able to explain that to this day.","s":"Fourteen points: the sun of a rice-growing people. Why fourteen? No one has been able to explain it yet."},{"tag":"🦚 Peacock feathers","q":"What's tucked between the points of the star?","a":"Triangular motifs that look like peacock feathers. From the center, concentric bands of patterns ripple outward; the Ngọc Lũ drum has sixteen bands in all."},{"tag":"🪶 People dressed as birds","q":"What are those people in feathered headdresses doing?","a":"They're dressed up as birds; some carry spears, some play the khèn, a bamboo mouth organ. Archaeologists agree this is a ritual with music. Some read it as a festival praying for a good harvest; others think it's a funeral rite.","s":"A ritual with music: people dressed as birds, holding spears, playing mouth organs. Harvest festival or funeral? The debate goes on."},{"tag":"🥁 Drums on a drum","q":"Can you see the drums pictured right on the face of the drum?","a":"A row of drums, with someone pounding down on them with a long pole. That's direct evidence: bronze drums were instruments for community rituals."},{"tag":"🛖 Boat-roofed stilt house","q":"What does this house with a boat-shaped roof tell us?","a":"It's a stilt house with a curved, boat-shaped roof and bird heads on the gables. Some believe it was a ritual house of the Lạc Việt, the ancient Vietnamese, and a distant ancestor of the village communal house roof."},{"tag":"🌾 Pounding rice","q":"What's this pair holding pestles doing?","a":"Pounding rice. The pestles even have feathers attached, so many think this may be part of a harvest ritual of wet-rice farmers."},{"tag":"🦌 Herds of deer","q":"What animals are on the outer bands?","a":"Herds of deer, along with flocks of flying birds, all heading in the same direction."},{"tag":"🐦 The Lạc bird?","q":"What are the long-beaked birds on the outermost band?","a":"People usually call them Lạc birds, but that's just a conventional name. Some say they're egrets, herons or cranes; others say cormorants. The Ngọc Lũ drum has thirty-six of them: eighteen in flight and eighteen standing.","s":"They're usually called Lạc birds, but the real species is still debated. The Ngọc Lũ drum has thirty-six of them."},{"tag":"↺ Counterclockwise","q":"Have you noticed that everything moves in the same direction?","a":"People, deer and birds all circle the star counterclockwise. Why? There's still no agreed answer, only theories, such as a link with the way the sun's shadow moves."},{"tag":"🚣 Six boats","q":"What else is on the side of the drum?","a":"Six boats, carrying people in feathered headdresses, spearmen and a helmsman. Are they warships, racing boats, or boats carrying the souls of the dead? Researchers are still debating."}]},"quan-am-but-thap":{"title":"The Thousand-Eyed, Thousand-Armed Quan Âm of Bút Tháp Pagoda","artist":"An artisan surnamed Trương","date":"1656 (autumn of the Bính Thân year)","medium":"Wood, red lacquer and gilding","where":"Bút Tháp Pagoda (Ninh Phúc Temple), Bắc Ninh","place":{"name":"Bút Tháp Pagoda","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["Buddhist sculpture","Lê dynasty","national treasure","Quan Âm"],"hook":"How many arms does this statue have? Ask ten people, and you might get ten different numbers.","intro":"The Thousand-Eyed, Thousand-Armed Quan Âm, Bodhisattva of Compassion, at Bút Tháp Pagoda in Bắc Ninh: red-lacquered, gilded wood, carved in {1656|sixteen fifty-six}.","openers":["Why is there an eye in the palm of every small hand on this statue?","This statue has a rare detail: it tells us the year it was carved, and even who carved it.","Stop and look at the hands joined before the chest: the two palms don't actually touch."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours standing before a single statue…","end":"Nearly a thousand hands, each with its own eye: a lesson in compassion that knows how to see and how to act. Now you understand why people can stand before this statue for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Benjamin Smith (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Khangoii (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Khangoii (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"📏 3.7 meters","q":"How tall is the statue?","a":"Nearly four meters, including the pedestal. It was carved in {1656|sixteen fifty-six}, during the Restored Lê period, and was named a National Treasure in the very first round, in {2012|twenty twelve}."},{"tag":"🖐️ Nearly 1,000 hands","q":"So how many arms are there?","a":"Most sources count forty-two large arms, plus a halo of nearly a thousand small hands arranged in layers. Every book counts differently: {952|nine hundred fifty-two}, {958|nine hundred fifty-eight}… Because 'a thousand' here isn't a count; it's a symbol of perfect completeness.","s":"About forty-two large arms and nearly a thousand small hands; every book counts differently, because 'a thousand' here is a symbol of completeness."},{"tag":"👁️ An eye in each palm","q":"Did you notice there's an eye in the palm of each small hand?","a":"The eye is knowing; the hand is doing. Only by knowing can you act rightly, and only by acting do you come to know deeply. A thousand eyes, a thousand hands: compassion that sees everything and acts to help all living beings."},{"tag":"✖️ 40 × 25 = 1,000","q":"How do forty-two large arms add up to a thousand?","a":"By the convention for thousand-armed statues: apart from the two joined hands, each of the remaining forty arms saves beings in twenty-five realms. Forty times twenty-five: exactly one thousand."},{"tag":"🙂 Eleven faces","q":"Why does the statue have as many as eleven faces?","a":"The faces are stacked in tiers, each with its own expression, symbolizing how Quan Âm appears to help in every situation. A Tibetan legend says that when the vow to save all beings wavered, Quan Âm's body shattered, and Amitabha Buddha pieced it back together with eleven faces and a thousand arms.","s":"Each face is different: Quan Âm appears in every situation. A Tibetan legend says Quan Âm's body once shattered, and Amitabha Buddha rebuilt it with eleven faces."},{"tag":"🧘 Amitabha","q":"Who's sitting at the very top?","a":"A small statue of Amitabha Buddha, placed at the top in reverence to the Buddha."},{"tag":"🙏 Hollow joined palms","q":"Why don't the hands joined before the chest press tightly together?","a":"The palms don't touch, leaving an empty space between them. One researcher calls this gesture hư tâm hiệp chưởng, 'joined palms with a hollow center', and links it to Tibetan statues, where the palms sometimes hold a jewel symbolizing the mind set on enlightenment."},{"tag":"✨ Red lacquer and gold","q":"What expression does the main face wear?","a":"Gentle, with eyes cast down. Its coat of red lacquer and gold leaf, done with traditional Vietnamese lacquer techniques, makes the face seem to glow in the darkness of the main hall."},{"tag":"🐉 Dragon under the lotus","q":"Who's the creature beneath the lotus throne?","a":"According to some sources, it's a Dragon King holding up the lotus throne, a guardian of the Buddha's teachings. The lotus throne stands for purity rising out of the mud."},{"tag":"✍️ Inscribed in 1656","q":"Where is this statue signed?","a":"An inscription on the statue says it was made in the autumn of the Bính Thân year, that is, {1656|sixteen fifty-six}, along with the carver's name; later studies attribute it to an artisan surnamed Trương. An ancient statue that records both when and by whom it was carved is a real rarity."}]},"chua-mot-cot":{"title":"The One Pillar Pagoda","artist":"Built under Emperor Lý Thái Tông; the current structure was rebuilt under architect Nguyễn Bá Lăng (1955)","date":"1049 (rebuilt in 1955)","medium":"Wood, stone, tile","where":"Chùa Một Cột Street, Ba Đình, Hanoi","place":{"name":"One Pillar Pagoda","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Lý dynasty","pagoda","Hanoi","Buddhist architecture"],"hook":"A pagoda standing on just one pillar. Its story begins with a dream, and once, it was blown apart in a single night.","intro":"The One Pillar Pagoda in Hanoi, formally named Diên Hựu. The first pagoda on this site was built in {1049|ten forty-nine}, under Emperor Lý Thái Tông.","openers":["Did you know the One Pillar Pagoda has a formal name that means 'lasting blessings'?","Here's a detail from this pagoda's history: a huge bell once lay out in a rice field, where turtles made their home.","Try to spot the pair of dragons facing the moon on the roof, and the four dragon heads at its four corners."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours gazing at one tiny pagoda…","end":"A dream, a pillar, a lotus in a pond. Now you understand why this tiny pagoda has stood in the hearts of Hanoians for nearly a thousand years.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0)","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Firmin-André Salles, 1896","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🌙 A dream in 1049","q":"Why would a pagoda stand on a single pillar?","a":"According to the Complete Annals of Đại Việt, an old royal chronicle, in {1049|ten forty-nine} Emperor Lý Thái Tông dreamed that Quan Âm, seated on a lotus throne, led him up onto a platform. His courtiers saw it as a bad omen. The monk Thiền Tuệ advised him to raise a stone pillar from the ground and set Quan Âm's lotus throne on top, just as in the dream.","s":"Emperor Lý Thái Tông dreamed of Quan Âm on a lotus throne; the monk Thiền Tuệ advised raising a stone pillar with the lotus throne on top, as in the dream."},{"tag":"🪷 Diên Hựu","q":"What's the pagoda's real name?","a":"Diên Hựu, meaning 'lasting blessings': when it was finished, monks walked around it chanting sutras for the emperor's long life. The square shrine on the pillar is called Liên Hoa Đài, the Lotus Platform."},{"tag":"🏛️ One pillar","q":"How big is this pillar?","a":"About {1.2 meters|one point two meters} across and roughly four meters tall from the ground. The pillar is the stem of a lotus rising out of the pond."},{"tag":"🌿 Like a flower's sepals","q":"What are the slanted wooden braces under the shrine floor for?","a":"They hold up the shrine, fanning out like the sepals that cradle a lotus flower."},{"tag":"🐉 Dragons facing the moon","q":"What's on the roof?","a":"Four sweeping, upturned corners shaped like dragon heads, and on the ridge, a pair of dragons facing the moon."},{"tag":"◻️ Linh Chiểu Pond","q":"Why is the pond square?","a":"Linh Chiểu Pond is square, about sixteen meters on each side. One reading says the square pond stands for the earth; that's one interpretation. The more familiar meaning: a pure lotus rising out of the mud."},{"tag":"🗺️ A Lý-era complex","q":"Was the pagoda this small back in the Lý dynasty?","a":"No. In {1105|eleven oh five}, Emperor Lý Nhân Tông had it expanded: digging Linh Chiểu Pond and adding Bích Trì Pond, galleries, bridges and a stupa. One researcher sees the whole complex as a mandala, a model of the Buddhist universe."},{"tag":"📷 In 1896","q":"What's special about this photo from {1896|eighteen ninety-six}?","a":"This is the One Pillar Pagoda in a photo taken in {1896|eighteen ninety-six}. The pagoda we see today is not that same building."},{"tag":"💥 1954","q":"What happened one night in {September 1954|September nineteen fifty-four}?","a":"Just before French troops withdrew from Hanoi, explosives were planted at the pagoda, bringing down the shrine. In {1955|nineteen fifty-five}, architect Nguyễn Bá Lăng led its reconstruction, following the Nguyễn-dynasty design. The pagoda you see today is that rebuilt version."},{"tag":"🐢 The Quy Điền Bell","q":"Why did the pagoda's bell end up in a rice field, home to turtles?","a":"In {1080|ten eighty}, under the Lý dynasty, the pagoda cast the Giác Thế bell. Too heavy to hang, it was left out in a rice field, where turtles moved in: hence the name Quy Điền, 'turtle field'. In {1426|fourteen twenty-six}, Ming troops from China broke it up for its bronze to make weapons.","card":{"big":"🔔","sub":"The Quy Điền “Turtle Field” Bell"}}]},"tien-quan-ca":{"title":"Army March","artist":"Văn Cao (1923–1995)","date":"Late 1944","medium":"March","where":"Written in an attic room at 45 Nguyễn Thượng Hiền Street, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Nguyễn Thượng Hiền Street (the attic where it was written)","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["national anthem","march","August Revolution","Văn Cao"],"hook":"The song an entire nation stands at attention for at every flag ceremony was written in a cramped attic, during the famine of {1944|nineteen forty-four}, by a twenty-one-year-old who had never held a gun.","intro":"Army March, Vietnam's national anthem, by composer Văn Cao. This video doesn't quote the lyrics; it just tells the story behind the song.","openers":["Did you know Vietnam's national anthem was never written to be a national anthem?","Here's a detail from the pottery village of Bát Tràng: the very first printing of this song was corrected with lime juice.","Next time you hear the anthem, listen to the very first note: it was once shortened to make the song punchier."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why millions of people can be moved to tears singing one song together…","end":"A march as short as a bugle call, born amid hunger and cold, written backwards on a slab of stone so it could be printed. Now you understand why, every time it rings out, it still brings people to tears.","img":[{"by":"Photo: rusticus80 (CC BY-SA 2.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"by":"Photo: P. Hughes (CC BY 4.0)","lic":"CC BY 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Liftold (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Greenknight dv (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🕯️ Amid a famine","q":"How did this song come into the world?","a":"In late {1944|nineteen forty-four}, famine was spreading across northern Vietnam. Văn Cao was twenty-one and penniless; some nights he burned his own manuscripts for warmth. He recalled walking through streets where people were dying of hunger, and that night he wrote the first notes.","s":"Late {1944|nineteen forty-four}, in the middle of a famine: a twenty-one-year-old burned his manuscripts for warmth and wrote the first notes.","card":{"big":"1944","sub":"The attic at 45 Nguyễn Thượng Hiền, Hanoi"}},{"tag":"🎺 Like a bugle call","q":"Was this song written to be a national anthem?","a":"No. It was the first task a cadre of the Việt Minh, the independence movement, gave him: write a song for a military training class. He had never been to the resistance zone, so he wrote something soldiers could sing right away, short and sharp like a bugle call.","card":{"big":"🎺","sub":"A song for a military training class"}},{"tag":"🍋 Backwards on stone","q":"Where was the first printing made?","a":"In the pottery village of Bát Tràng, in {November 1944|November nineteen forty-four}, in the underground newspaper Độc Lập, 'Independence'. Văn Cao himself wrote the song backwards onto stone for lithographic printing, and used lime juice instead of acid to correct the plate. That stone was hidden in the village pond and only pulled out in {1984|nineteen eighty-four}.","s":"In the pottery village of Bát Tràng: Văn Cao wrote the song backwards on a lithography stone, using lime juice instead of acid."},{"tag":"🏯 Tân Trào, August 1945","q":"When did it become the national anthem?","a":"In mid-{August 1945|August nineteen forty-five}, at Tân Trào, President Hồ Chí Minh chose the Army March because it was short, strong and easy to sing. In {1946|nineteen forty-six}, the first National Assembly officially adopted it, and the first Constitution wrote it in."},{"tag":"🌳 The Tân Trào banyan","q":"Where did soldiers first sing this song?","a":"According to the composer's family, the Liberation Army unit sang the Army March at its send-off ceremony beneath the banyan tree of Tân Trào."},{"tag":"🏛️ Opera House, 8/17/1945","q":"Why is the Hanoi Opera House tied to this song?","a":"On {August 17, 1945|August seventeenth, nineteen forty-five}, a rally at the Opera House suddenly changed course: a red flag with a yellow star unfurled from the balcony, and thousands of people sang together. Văn Cao, though ill, still made it there, and he broke down in tears."},{"tag":"🎼 Two notes","q":"Did you know the opening note was once changed?","a":"Before {September 2, 1945|September second, nineteen forty-five}, conductor Đinh Ngọc Liên and Văn Cao agreed to change two notes, shortening the opening note to make the song more vigorous. The version sung today is that revised one.","card":{"big":"♪","sub":"The opening note was shortened"}},{"tag":"🏁 Nearly 1,500 entries","q":"Why is only the first verse sung?","a":"The Army March has two verses, but since {1955|nineteen fifty-five}, only the first has been used officially. In {1981|nineteen eighty-one}, nearly fifteen hundred songs were entered in a contest for a new anthem, but in the end the National Assembly kept the Army March."},{"tag":"🤝 Donated in 2016","q":"Who does the national anthem belong to?","a":"On {July 15, 2016|July fifteenth, twenty sixteen}, Văn Cao's family officially donated the song to the State. As the composer himself once said, the song had long since stopped being his alone; it belonged to every Vietnamese person.","card":{"big":"2016","sub":"The song belongs to everyone"}}]},"quan-ho-nguoi-oi-nguoi-o-dung-ve":{"title":"Quan họ: “Dear Friend, Please Stay”","artist":"Quan họ folk song (popular version arranged by composer Xuân Tứ in 1958)","date":"A centuries-old tradition; arranged in 1958","medium":"Folk song, call-and-response singing without accompaniment","where":"49 original quan họ villages on both banks of the Cầu River, Bắc Ninh","place":{"name":"Diềm village (Viêm Xá), where Vua Bà, founder of quan họ, is worshipped","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["quan họ","UNESCO","folk song","Bắc Ninh"],"hook":"They sing to each other all night long, call each other 'big brother' and 'big sister', and hold each other dear. But there's a village rule: quan họ partners never marry each other.","intro":"Quan họ folk songs from Bắc Ninh in northern Vietnam, inscribed by UNESCO in {2009|two thousand nine}. This video doesn't quote any lyrics; it just tells the story behind them.","openers":["Did you know this beloved quan họ farewell song was reworked by a composer in {1958|nineteen fifty-eight}?","A detail about the nón quai thao, the wide silk-strapped hat: in the old days, quan họ singers sometimes hid their faces behind it.","Next time you hear quan họ, listen for the nảy: a sound caught for an instant in the throat, then released."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can sit and listen to quan họ all night long…","end":"A night of singing without instruments, a bond never allowed to become a marriage, and a plea for the guests to stay as day breaks. Now you understand why people can listen to quan họ their whole lives.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Chrisvomberg (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Viethavvh (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Vanminhhanoi (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🤝 Quan họ partners","q":"Who are the liền anh and liền chị?","a":"They're the quan họ singers: liền anh are the men, liền chị the women. A group of men from one village bonds with a group of women from another, and only groups bonded this way sing together. They sing in call and response: one side offers a melody, and the other answers with a similar tune but different words."},{"tag":"🏘️ 49 original villages","q":"Why are there forty-nine original quan họ villages?","a":"The forty-nine villages lie along both banks of the Cầu River. Legend tells of Vua Bà, the Lady King, a daughter of the legendary Hùng kings, who taught the people of Diềm village to plant rice, weave silk and sing. One version says she and forty-nine people became sworn brothers and sisters, then spread out to teach quan họ.","card":{"big":"49","sub":"original quan họ villages on both banks of the Cầu River"}},{"tag":"💔 The no-marriage rule","q":"Why aren't quan họ partners allowed to marry?","a":"It's a village custom; some places even wrote it into their village charters, and UNESCO mentions it too, though some couples still broke the rule. One elderly quan họ singer believes it's this very rule that gives quan họ melodies their gentle, lingering sadness.","card":{"big":"💔","sub":"Loving, but never marrying"}},{"tag":"🌙 All-night singing","q":"How long does a quan họ singing night last?","a":"It can run from dusk until dawn, about ten hours, with no microphones and no instruments. A singing night must have all three stages: lề lối, the old formal melodies; giọng vặt, freer songs; and finally giã bạn, the farewell, as dawn approaches.","card":{"big":"7 p.m. → 5 a.m.","sub":"One singing night"}},{"tag":"🎙️ Vang, rền, nền, nảy","q":"What do vang, rền, nền and nảy mean?","a":"Vang is a voice that carries far. Rền is a steady vibration that never runs out of breath. Nảy is a sound caught very quickly in the throat, then released. As for nền, people still argue about it.","card":{"big":"Vang · Rền · Nền · Nảy","sub":"Four words for the quan họ voice"}},{"tag":"👒 The quai thao hat","q":"What's this hat, nearly eighty centimeters wide, for?","a":"It's the nón quai thao, with silk straps. In the old days, duet singers sometimes hid their faces behind it while singing."},{"tag":"👘 The áo mớ ba","q":"What do the liền chị wear?","a":"The áo mớ ba: three layered tunics, a peach-pink bodice, a crow's-beak headscarf, and a sash tied like a flower petal at the front."},{"tag":"🎩 Gauze robe and turban","q":"And the liền anh?","a":"A black gauze robe over a long tunic, a pleated turban, a silk umbrella, and humble manners: they call themselves em, 'the younger one', and their partners 'big brother', 'big sister'. No one ranks above anyone else."},{"tag":"🛶 Festival singing","q":"Is singing on boats the old-style quan họ?","a":"Not really. Singing on boats is mostly a festival-day activity. The traditional way is hát canh: at night, indoors, between quan họ groups who have bonded with each other."},{"tag":"🎶 Xuân Tứ, 1958","q":"Is “Dear Friend, Please Stay” really an old song?","a":"Not quite. Today's popular version was reworked in {1958|nineteen fifty-eight} by composer Xuân Tứ from an old quan họ song. He trimmed it down, keeping only the parting, the last words of advice and the longing, and pushed the refrain higher so the plea to stay sounds even more heartfelt.","card":{"big":"1958","sub":"Composer Xuân Tứ reworks the song"}},{"tag":"🌅 Farewell at dawn","q":"Why is the farewell song so sad?","a":"Because it rises after a long night of singing together, as day breaks: the hosts beg their guests to stay and promise to meet again, knowing that by custom, this bond can never become a marriage."}]},"nguyen-gia-tri-binh-phong":{"title":"Screen: “Young Women and Landscape”","artist":"Nguyễn Gia Trí (1908–1993)","date":"1939 (per Decision 2089/QĐ-TTg; some sources give 1944)","medium":"Two-sided lacquer painting on eight hinged lacquered wooden panels: vermilion, black lacquer, gold, silver, eggshell, cockroach-wing lacquer","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, 66 Nguyễn Thái Học, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["lacquer painting","folding screen","Indochinese art","national treasure"],"hook":"On one side, seven women stroll through a sun-drenched garden; on the other, a country garden of giant elephant-ear plants, banana trees and bamboo. Why would a single screen hold two completely different worlds?","intro":"Young Women and Landscape, a two-sided lacquer screen by Nguyễn Gia Trí, dated {1939|nineteen thirty-nine} in its National Treasure dossier. It's now in the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Try counting how many women there are on this side of the screen, and who's the youngest.","Stop and look at the vertical seams that divide the picture into eight panels: this is no painting made to hang on a wall.","Did you know the other side of this screen shows a plant that Vietnamese families still cook into sour soup?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours walking around a single lacquer screen…","end":"On one side, the stages of a woman's life in golden sunlight; on the other, a country garden of dọc mùng, banana trees and bamboo. Now you understand why people can walk around this screen for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: VnExpress","lic":"© Nguyễn Gia Trí"},{"by":"Photo: VnExpress","lic":"© Nguyễn Gia Trí"},{"by":"Photo: Hoangkid, Wikimedia Commons, 2012","lic":"© Nguyễn Gia Trí"}],"steps":[{"tag":"👩 Seven women","q":"How many women are there on this side of the screen?","a":"Seven, each at a different age; the youngest is the short-haired little girl holding a sprig of flowers on the right. According to the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, they stand for the stages of a woman's life, so walking along the eight panels is like walking through a whole lifetime.","s":"Seven, each a different age: according to the museum, they're the stages of a woman's life."},{"tag":"☀️ Gold, sun and clouds","q":"Why does the whole garden seem bathed in sunlight?","a":"Because the background is covered in gold. Across it, a few curling bands of cloud drift among huge banana leaves. The gold in lacquer is real gold leaf, laid on paper-thin, then coated with lacquer and polished, so it glows from deep inside."},{"tag":"🌿 The dọc mùng side","q":"Flip to the other side, and what do you see?","a":"A country garden on a dark background, centered on dọc mùng, a giant elephant-ear plant familiar in the Vietnamese countryside, mixed with clumps of banana and bamboo. Unlike the softness of the young women's side, this side feels sturdy and powerful.","s":"A country garden centered on dọc mùng plants, sturdy and strong, the very opposite of the soft young women's side."},{"tag":"🥚 Eggshell on the leaves","q":"What's so special about those big leaves?","a":"Look at the speckled white patches between the leaf veins: that's eggshell, crushed and set piece by piece into the lacquer, then coated with color and polished flat. Critic Nguyễn Hải Yến praised the eggshell on these taro leaves as masterly and supple.","s":"Those white patches are eggshell, which critic Nguyễn Hải Yến praised as masterly and supple."},{"tag":"🍌 Carved, gilded bananas","q":"And how were the banana trees made?","a":"Look at the fine parallel veins on the banana leaves. According to {VnExpress International|V N Express International}, the banana trees were delicately carved into the lacquer, then gilded. Carving, raised relief, gilding, then polishing: one side of this screen gathers almost every trick of the lacquer craft."},{"tag":"🪟 Eight panels, two sides","q":"Why is this picture split into eight panels?","a":"Because it's a screen: eight lacquered wooden panels joined by hinges, so it can stand upright in the middle of a room. Both sides were made with the utmost care, so as you walk around it, you get to see two separate paintings."},{"tag":"🧭 Đà Lạt, 1978","q":"How did this screen end up in the museum?","a":"According to critic Nguyễn Hải Yến, in the winter of {1978|nineteen seventy-eight}, a team of specialists collecting paintings in the south came across this screen at the Đà Lạt Guest House, once a palace of Bảo Đại, Vietnam's last emperor. It was quickly moved to the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, and another carved lacquer screen of similar size took its old place.","s":"According to critic Nguyễn Hải Yến, it was found in {1978|nineteen seventy-eight} at a guest house in Đà Lạt, once Bảo Đại's palace, then moved to Hanoi.","card":{"big":"1978","sub":"Found at a Đà Lạt guest house, once Bảo Đại's palace"}},{"tag":"🎨 Red, black, gold, silver","q":"What did the painter use to create this side of the screen?","a":"Vermilion, black lacquer, gold, silver, eggshell and cockroach-wing lacquer, a clear lacquer with a reddish-brown tint. He laid on one layer at a time, let it dry, then polished it back so the layers beneath showed through. Tô Ngọc Vân once wrote that with Nguyễn Gia Trí's experiments, Vietnamese lacquer stopped being just a decorative craft."},{"tag":"📅 Painted when?","q":"When was this screen made?","a":"The {2017|twenty seventeen} decision naming it a National Treasure says {1939|nineteen thirty-nine}, when Nguyễn Gia Trí was at his peak and the art world ranked painters as first Trí, second Lân, third Vân, fourth Cẩn. But one article says {1944|nineteen forty-four}, and critic Nguyễn Hải Yến once guessed even later, judging by the gold background. The birth year of this treasure is still an open question.","card":{"big":"1939?","sub":"The treasure dossier says 1939; one source says 1944"}}]},"nguyen-sang-ket-nap-dang-dien-bien-phu":{"title":"Party Admission Ceremony at Điện Biên Phủ","artist":"Nguyễn Sáng (1923–1988)","date":"1963","medium":"Lacquer","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, 66 Nguyễn Thái Học, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["lacquer painting","Điện Biên Phủ","national treasure","Nguyễn Sáng"],"hook":"A ceremony held inside a trench, and the soldier joining the Party has his head wrapped in a white bandage. When this lacquer painting first came out, some even said its soldiers looked crude.","intro":"Party Admission Ceremony at Điện Biên Phủ, lacquer, by Nguyễn Sáng, {1963|nineteen sixty-three}. A National Treasure, now in the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Try counting how many soldiers are in this trench, and how many of them are wounded.","Stop and look at the soldier with the white bandage in the middle: he's the one being admitted.","Why is this lacquer painting covered in earthy browns, instead of the brilliant vermilion and gold of decorative lacquer?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours in front of a single lacquer painting…","end":"A cramped trench, a bandaged soldier and the brown of the earth. Now you understand why people can stand in front of this lacquer painting for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"© Nguyễn Sáng"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🕳️ A trench ceremony","q":"Why is the ceremony taking place in a cramped trench?","a":"Painter Hoàng Đình Tài points out that admission ceremonies were usually held behind the lines, once the fighting was over. Nguyễn Sáng instead set it right in a combat trench, between two battles. Eight soldiers are squeezed into a tight, suffocating space, as if the battle were still raging just outside.","s":"Admission ceremonies were usually held behind the lines, after the fighting. Nguyễn Sáng set this one right in a combat trench, between two battles."},{"tag":"🩹 The new member","q":"Who is the soldier with the white bandage in the middle?","a":"He's the very man being admitted into the Communist Party. Wounded, his head bandaged, he still grips his gun firmly, standing at attention, eyes on the Party flag on the trench wall in the top right corner. Researcher Nguyễn Hải Yến calls this the painting's climax: a wounded soldier being admitted, who may lose his life right afterwards.","s":"He's the one being admitted into the Party: wounded, head bandaged, still gripping his gun, eyes on the flag on the trench wall."},{"tag":"🤝 The handshake","q":"What are the two soldiers on the right doing?","a":"According to the People's Army newspaper, they're the secretary of the Party cell and a man on lookout duty. Where the two groups meet, two hands clasp: a resolute handshake linking them to the group of three in the middle. Thanks to that gesture, the soldiers in the painting are bound together into one solid mass."},{"tag":"🚑 Holding up a comrade","q":"Who's helping whom in the left corner?","a":"A soldier is holding up a badly wounded comrade who can barely stand. Of the eight soldiers in the painting, two are wounded. The painter doesn't hide the cost of war, and according to the People's Army newspaper, that was an image people at the time usually shied away from."},{"tag":"🏃 Off to battle","q":"Where is the little soldier half-hidden at the back going?","a":"Into battle. Half-hidden behind the men at the ceremony, with only his hat and shoulders showing, is a fighter rushing to his combat position, according to the People's Army newspaper. The ceremony goes on, yet the fighting hasn't stopped for a moment. That tiny detail tells us the ceremony had to be quick, squeezed in between two battles."},{"tag":"🟫 The color of earth","q":"Why is the whole painting covered in earthy brown?","a":"A yellowish earth-brown spreads across the whole painting, with just a few touches of red. One critic reads that brown as the color of the land that farmers plow and protect. The materials are still traditional Vietnamese lacquer: vermilion, black, gold, silver, eggshell, but the gold and silver are used very sparingly.","s":"A yellowish earth-brown with touches of red. One critic reads it as the color of the land farmers plow and protect."},{"tag":"✏️ Simplified lines","q":"Why do the figures look as if they're built from flat shapes?","a":"Nguyễn Sáng doesn't imitate real light. He lines the figures up side by side into one solid block, pares down the lines, uses flat areas of color, and outlines the forms in black and brown. Painter Lương Xuân Đoàn noted that lacquer had long stuck to vermilion grounds and gold outlines, while this bold, sturdy style created soldiers of Điện Biên who feel true to life."},{"tag":"⏳ Nine years","q":"Why did the painting only appear nine years after the victory?","a":"According to painter Lương Xuân Đoàn, Nguyễn Sáng was once reprimanded for going to Điện Biên without sketching the soldiers. He carried those images in his heart for years. The first sketch dates from {1959|nineteen fifty-nine}, and only in {1963|nineteen sixty-three}, at the age of forty, did he finish the lacquer painting.","card":{"big":"1954 → 1963","sub":"Nine years after the victory, it became a painting"}},{"tag":"😮 Once criticized","q":"When it first came out, was the painting praised right away?","a":"Not exactly. When it was first shown, it caused a stir, with sharply divided opinions. Some said the soldiers looked crude. But these were the true faces of barefoot peasants, and over time, it's precisely that rustic, sturdy quality that gave the painting its value.","s":"When first shown, it caused a stir, and some said the soldiers looked crude. Over time, that rustic quality is exactly what gave the painting its value."}]},"nguyen-sang-thanh-nien-thanh-dong":{"title":"The Youth of the Bronze Fortress","artist":"Nguyễn Sáng (1923–1988)","date":"1967–1978","medium":"Lacquer","where":"Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of Fine Arts, 97A Phó Đức Chính, Hồ Chí Minh City","place":{"name":"Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hồ Chí Minh City"},"tags":["lacquer painting","Saigon","youth","national treasure"],"hook":"Two soldiers stand with their backs to you, guns pointed at a wall of people. The man at the front of that wall has no weapon, only two hands clutching the front of his shirt.","intro":"The Youth of the Bronze Fortress, lacquer, by Nguyễn Sáng, painted from {1967|nineteen sixty-seven} to {1978|nineteen seventy-eight}. A National Treasure, now in the Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Have you noticed that both soldiers in this painting have their backs to us?","Try to find an English word written near the top edge of the painting, among the heads.","Stop and look at the crowd on the right: only the front row is painted in full; behind them, there are only faces."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours in front of one huge lacquer painting…","end":"Two soldiers, a wall of people, and eleven years of waiting between Hanoi and Saigon. Now you understand why people can stand in front of this lacquer painting for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of Fine Arts (via VnExpress)","lic":"© Nguyễn Sáng"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🪖 Backs to us","q":"Why do the two soldiers have their backs to us?","a":"Two tall American soldiers in steel helmets, ammunition pouches around their waists, high boots, guns aimed at the crowd. We can't see their faces. It's as if we're standing right behind them, looking straight into the faces of the young people. That's one way to read the composition; you can check it for yourself.","s":"Two tall American soldiers stand with their backs to us, guns aimed at the crowd. We never see their faces, only those of the young people."},{"tag":"🧱 A wall of people","q":"What is a bronze fortress, and where is it in this painting?","a":"A bronze fortress is a fortress with walls of bronze, so solid that nothing can break it. Look to the right, and you'll see it: young men and women standing pressed together like a wall. Only the front row is painted in full; behind them there are only faces, one after another, all looking at the two soldiers.","s":"A fortress of bronze: young men and women pressed together like a wall, all looking at the two soldiers."},{"tag":"🧍 The man in front","q":"What's special about the young man facing the two soldiers?","a":"He's the focal point of the painting. His face is set hard, his gaze resolute, his legs planted wide, and both hands grab the front of his shirt as if to tear it open. No weapon, just a firm stance, facing the barrel of a gun.","s":"Unarmed, he stands with legs wide apart, both hands gripping his shirt as if to rip it open, facing the gun barrels."},{"tag":"🔤 Words on the painting","q":"Why is there an English word in a lacquer painting?","a":"Near the top edge, among the heads, is the English word Home. According to Vietnam's Department of Fine Arts, the painting carries the words Go home and one Vietnamese word, like the slogans of the street protests in the cities of the South at the time. The painting was inspired by the movement of Saigon's school and university students in the {1960s|nineteen sixties} against the war and the presence of American troops."},{"tag":"✍️ Hanoi, Saigon","q":"How long did this painting take?","a":"Eleven years. According to the Department of Fine Arts, the painter wrote two abbreviated place names on it, Hanoi and Saigon, along with the years {1967|nineteen sixty-seven} to {1978|nineteen seventy-eight}. Nguyễn Sáng, a native of Mỹ Tho, had left the South back in {1940|nineteen forty}. He began the painting in Hanoi in {1967|nineteen sixty-seven}, while the country was still divided, and finished it eleven years later.","s":"Eleven years: he started it in Hanoi in {1967|nineteen sixty-seven}, while the country was still divided, and only finished in {1978|nineteen seventy-eight}.","card":{"big":"1967 → 1978","sub":"Begun in Hanoi, finished eleven years later"}},{"tag":"🎨 Flat areas, bold lines","q":"What set Nguyễn Sáng's lacquer apart from everyone else's?","a":"He tended to use few colors, flat areas, bold outlines and simplified forms to capture the spirit of his figures, and he didn't polish as deeply as Nguyễn Gia Trí. He also added blues and chlorophyll greens to the lacquer palette, so he could use lacquer to paint both everyday life and war."},{"tag":"📐 A huge format","q":"How big is the painting?","a":"It's {1.45 meters|one point four five meters} tall and {2.45 meters|two point four five meters} wide. In {2025|twenty twenty-five}, when Hồ Chí Minh City put its National Treasures on display at the History Museum, the original was too big to move, so it was presented on an interactive screen instead."},{"tag":"💰 2,000 đồng","q":"How did the painting get to the museum?","a":"On {December 1, 1980|December first, nineteen eighty}, Nguyễn Sáng sold the painting to the Museum of the Revolution in Hồ Chí Minh City for {2,000 đồng|two thousand dong}. It was once lent to East Germany for an exhibition, came back to Hanoi for his solo show in {1984|nineteen eighty-four}, and only in {2004|two thousand four} did it move to the city's Museum of Fine Arts.","s":"In {1980|nineteen eighty}, Nguyễn Sáng sold the painting to a museum in Hồ Chí Minh City for {2,000 đồng|two thousand dong}.","card":{"big":"2,000 đồng","sub":"The price paid by the museum in 1980"}},{"tag":"🏛️ Two treasures","q":"How many of Nguyễn Sáng's works are National Treasures?","a":"Two. The first is Party Admission Ceremony at Điện Biên Phủ, recognized in {2013|twenty thirteen}. The Youth of the Bronze Fortress was recognized in {2017|twenty seventeen}, in the sixth round of National Treasures. Both are among the six works for which he was posthumously awarded the Hồ Chí Minh Prize in {1996|nineteen ninety-six}.","card":{"big":"2013 · 2017","sub":"Nguyễn Sáng's two National Treasures"}}]},"nguyen-gia-tri-vuon-xuan":{"title":"Spring Garden of the Central, Southern and Northern Regions","artist":"Nguyễn Gia Trí (1908–1993)","date":"1969–1989","medium":"Lacquer: Vietnamese lacquer, vermilion, black lacquer, gold leaf, eggshell; nine joined lacquered panels","where":"Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of Fine Arts, 97A Phó Đức Chính, Hồ Chí Minh City","place":{"name":"Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hồ Chí Minh City"},"tags":["lacquer painting","Nguyễn Gia Trí","spring","national treasure"],"hook":"Twenty years to paint one garden. And among its crowd of young women, two little children are riding an animal that doesn't exist.","intro":"Spring Garden of the Central, Southern and Northern Regions, a lacquer painting by Nguyễn Gia Trí, painted from {1969|nineteen sixty-nine} to {1989|nineteen eighty-nine}. A National Treasure, now in the Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Try to find two little children in this garden riding a kỳ lân, a unicorn of legend, as if they'd just stepped out of a folk print.","Stop and look at the two edges: those columns of Chinese characters running top to bottom are two lines of poetry.","Did you know the tiny white specks all over this painting are eggshell, set in one piece at a time?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours wandering through one lacquer garden…","end":"A garden painted over twenty years, where young women from three regions, a folk-art kỳ lân and specks of eggshell all welcome the spring together. Now you understand why people can stand in front of this Spring Garden for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Hungneo, Wikimedia Commons (CC BY-SA 3.0), taken in 2011","lic":"© Nguyễn Gia Trí"}],"steps":[{"tag":"👭 Girls of three regions","q":"Where do the girls in the middle of this garden come from?","a":"From all three regions, Central, South and North, each in the traditional dress of her own region: a girl in yellow is dancing, two others sit playing instruments. Together they're off to a spring festival among pagodas and trees. Nguyễn Gia Trí began painting it in {1969|nineteen sixty-nine}, while the country was still at war, and many read the painting as a wish for peace and reunion.","s":"From all three regions, heading to a spring festival together; painted in wartime, it reads like a wish for peace and reunion."},{"tag":"🦄 A folk-art kỳ lân","q":"What animal are those two children riding?","a":"A white kỳ lân, a unicorn of legend, its mane and tail curling like tongues of flame: a creature that exists only in myth. The two children look as if they've stepped out of a folk print, riding the kỳ lân and romping right beside the young women. In the middle of a very modern painting, Nguyễn Gia Trí still tucked in a little of the soul of the old Lunar New Year prints.","s":"A kỳ lân, a creature of legend, and two children painted as if they'd just stepped out of a folk print."},{"tag":"💃 Dancers in white","q":"And what about the girls in white, whirling in a circle?","a":"This is one of two groups of young women dancing in a ring; the other is on the left side of the painting. They bring a breath of modern life in among the old shrine and the kỳ lân. That white is mostly eggshell, crushed and set in tiny pieces, and it makes the still garden suddenly spring into motion.","s":"Two groups of girls dancing in rings bring a modern air to the garden, and their white is mostly eggshell."},{"tag":"🛕 The old shrine","q":"What's the little building behind the group of girls?","a":"An old shrine, small but solemn, its curved roof tucked beneath the trees. The whole painting is {5.4 meters|five point four meters} long, made of nine joined panels, so people, trees and the shrine roof flow on and on, like a scroll slowly unrolling before your eyes."},{"tag":"📜 Two lines of poetry","q":"What do these Chinese characters along the edge say?","a":"It's a line of poetry in Chinese characters, and the other edge holds a second one. According to some sources, both lines are by Đào Duy Từ, a man of letters of the early seventeenth century. The verses describe the moon and the scent of flowers, like an inscription for the whole spring garden."},{"tag":"✨ Four lacquer colors","q":"How can a painting with just a few colors look so splendid?","a":"Basically just four colors: red is vermilion, black is black lacquer, yellow is real gold leaf, and white is eggshell, scattered into countless points of light. According to {VnExpress|V N Express}, the old master also had his students lightly polish the surface, harder here, gentler there, so the colors melt into one another."},{"tag":"⏳ Twenty years","q":"How long can it take to paint one picture?","a":"This one took twenty years, from {1969|nineteen sixty-nine} to {1989|nineteen eighty-nine}, and it was Nguyễn Gia Trí's last work. According to Việt Nam News, in {1988|nineteen eighty-eight} he suffered a stroke while painting, and had to ask his student Nguyễn Xuân Việt to lay a sheet of gold leaf onto the painting for him.","s":"Twenty years, from {1969|nineteen sixty-nine} to {1989|nineteen eighty-nine}; in {1988|nineteen eighty-eight} he had a stroke and had to ask a student to apply the gold for him.","card":{"big":"20 years","sub":"1969–1989, Nguyễn Gia Trí's last painting"}},{"tag":"💰 Bought for US$100,000","q":"How did the painting end up in the museum?","a":"In the early {1990s|nineteen nineties}, the Hồ Chí Minh City government bought the painting for {US$100,000|one hundred thousand US dollars}, then handed it over to the city's Museum of Fine Arts. In {2013|twenty thirteen}, it was recognized as a National Treasure.","card":{"big":"US$100,000","sub":"Bought by the city in the early 1990s"}},{"tag":"⚠️ The 2018 cleaning","q":"Why did this painting once leave the art world heartbroken?","a":"This photo was taken in {2011|twenty eleven}. According to press reports, in late {2018|twenty eighteen}, the painting was cleaned with dishwashing liquid, polishing powder and sandpaper. In {2019|twenty nineteen}, an inspection team from the Ministry of Culture concluded that about fifteen percent of the surface was damaged, eggshell patches like these were worn down and bleached white, and the soul of the painting had lost more than thirty percent."}]},"nguyen-tu-nghiem-giong":{"title":"Gióng","artist":"Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm (1922–2016)","date":"1990","medium":"Lacquer: vermilion ground, patches of gold and silver","where":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts, 66 Nguyễn Thái Học, Hanoi","place":{"name":"Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["lacquer painting","Saint Gióng","Đông Sơn","national treasure"],"hook":"The legendary hero of Gióng village has no bulging muscles here. Instead, his whole body is covered in patterns from Đông Sơn bronze drums.","intro":"Gióng, a lacquer painting by Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm from {1990|nineteen ninety}, winner of first prize at that year's National Fine Arts Exhibition. A National Treasure, now in the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts.","openers":["Try to find the small linked circles on Gióng's clothes: they come from the face of a bronze drum more than two thousand years old.","Have you noticed that Gióng and his horse are both charging to the right? Some say that, too, has its roots in the bronze drums.","Stop and look at the weapon Gióng carries: not a sword, not a spear, but an axe."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a single painting of Saint Gióng…","end":"A hero without muscles, wearing a whole Đông Sơn drum, riding a horse that seems to dance as it soars. Now you understand why people can stand in front of this Gióng for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"© Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm"},{"by":"Drawing of Đông Sơn drum patterns: Tom Mai","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🐎 A dancing, flying horse","q":"Is Gióng charging into battle, or flying away?","a":"This may be the moment Gióng rides his horse up to heaven after defeating the invaders. Painter Lương Xuân Đoàn observed that Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm didn't copy a real horse but breathed a soul into it, so the horse seems to dance as it carries Saint Gióng into the sky. From the hind leg at bottom left to the horse's head at top right, a rising diagonal sets the direction of flight.","s":"Maybe the moment Gióng flies up to heaven: the horse isn't copied from life, it seems to dance, rising along a diagonal."},{"tag":"🥁 Bronze drum patterns","q":"Where do the circles and sawtooth shapes on Gióng's body come from?","a":"From the face of Đông Sơn bronze drums. Instead of painting muscles, Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm covered Gióng's body and the horse's legs with the drums' classic motifs: circles linked by tangent lines, sawtooth patterns, angular S-shapes. The hero of legend wears an entire culture.","s":"From Đông Sơn bronze drums: linked circles, sawtooth bands and angular S-shapes cover Gióng's body instead of muscles."},{"tag":"🔍 Compare with a drum","q":"On a real bronze drum, where do those patterns appear?","a":"In the narrow bands around the star at the center of the drum's face, between the bands of people, birds and deer. This is a reconstruction drawing, but you can still make out the chains of linked circles and the bands of geometric patterns. Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm lifted them off a drum face more than two thousand years old and ran them down Gióng's tunic."},{"tag":"🪓 A bronze axe","q":"In the legend, Gióng fights with an iron rod and bamboo, so what weapon does he carry here?","a":"A bronze axe with a curved, fan-shaped blade, covered in Đông Sơn-style circles and spirals. According to an analysis published in {VnExpress|V N Express}, the painter chose an axe blade, a working tool of the ancient people, rather than a dagger or a spearhead. As if to say that those who rise up to defend the village are the very ones who clear the forest and work the fields.","s":"A bronze axe: according to an analysis in {VnExpress|V N Express}, a working tool of the ancient people, chosen instead of a dagger or spearhead."},{"tag":"🛡️ A chest shield","q":"What's that on Gióng's chest?","a":"A heart guard, a warrior's chest shield. According to the analysis in {VnExpress|V N Express}, the red spirals on it are a pair of entwined water dragons, a Đông Sơn motif. The Đông Sơn people really did have bronze heart guards like this, such as one decorated with water dragons found in Ninh Bình."},{"tag":"➡️ Always facing right","q":"Have you noticed that both rider and horse face to the right?","a":"In Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm's paintings of Gióng, the main figure always faces right. Some link that to the circling movement of people, birds and animals on the faces of bronze drums. Some have even found the golden ratio in the way he divided up the picture."},{"tag":"🔷 Cubism, village carving","q":"Why do rider and horse look as if they were assembled from geometric pieces?","a":"Look at the horse's hind legs: nothing but angular blocks fitted together. Nguyễn Tư Nghiêm borrowed the language of Western Cubism and Futurism, building rider and horse from geometric shapes and setting patches of gold, silver and gray on a vermilion-red ground. But the roots remain Vietnamese: all his life, he drew inspiration from the carvings of communal houses and pagodas, and from ancient patterns."},{"tag":"🏆 First prize, 1990","q":"How did the painting come to the museum?","a":"In {1990|nineteen ninety}, Gióng won first prize at the National Fine Arts Exhibition, and the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts bought it right afterwards. The museum still keeps the purchase notice, with the painter's handwritten note and his signature agreeing to the sale. It's considered the most successful of his Gióng series, and in {2017|twenty seventeen}, it became a National Treasure.","s":"First prize at the {1990|nineteen ninety} National Fine Arts Exhibition; the museum bought it at once and still keeps the notice he signed agreeing to sell.","card":{"big":"1st prize 1990","sub":"Bought by the museum right after the national exhibition"}},{"tag":"✈️ Off to England, 2013","q":"What was this painting once insured for?","a":"In {2013|twenty thirteen}, Gióng traveled to England as the centerpiece of a festival at the Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology in Cambridge, insured for as much as {US$1 million|one million US dollars}. More than {2,200|two thousand two hundred} visitors came to see it in ten days.","card":{"big":"US$1 million","sub":"Its insured value for the 2013 exhibition in England"}}]},"thap-dong-dao-thinh":{"title":"The Đào Thịnh Bronze Jar","artist":"People of the Đông Sơn culture","date":"About 2,000–2,500 years ago (Đông Sơn culture)","medium":"Cast bronze, with a lid","where":"National Museum of Vietnamese History, Hanoi (found at Đào Thịnh, Trấn Yên, in the former Yên Bái province, now part of Lào Cai)","place":{"name":"National Museum of Vietnamese History","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Đông Sơn","bronze jar","fertility","national treasure"],"hook":"Four tiny pairs of men and women on the lid of a bronze jar more than two thousand years old. What were the Đông Sơn people trying to say with such daring images?","intro":"The Đào Thịnh bronze jar, from the Đông Sơn culture, some two thousand to two and a half thousand years old, now kept at the National Museum of Vietnamese History. A National Treasure since {2012|twenty twelve}.","openers":["Try to find the tiny figures on the rim of this jar's lid: there are four pairs in all.","Why would the Đông Sơn people cast a jar nearly a meter tall, and give it a lid too?","Stop and look at the bands of patterns around the jar's shoulder: rows of triangles, diamonds, chains of linked circles."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a single bronze jar…","end":"Flying birds, boats carrying people in feathered headdresses, and four couples praying for fertility: the whole spiritual world of the Đông Sơn people on a single jar. Now you understand why people can spend hours looking at one bronze jar.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · National Museum of Vietnamese History","lic":"CC0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🏺 The biggest jar","q":"How big is this jar?","a":"Nearly a meter tall, with a mouth more than sixty centimeters across, a cylindrical body tapering toward the base, and a domed lid on top. Of the hundreds of Đông Sơn jars found so far, it's considered the largest and the finest.","s":"Nearly a meter tall, it's considered the largest and finest of the hundreds of Đông Sơn jars found so far."},{"tag":"🏞️ By the Red River","q":"Where was this jar found?","a":"On the banks of the Red River, in Đào Thịnh commune, in the former province of Yên Bái, now part of Lào Cai. It was discovered by chance in the early {1960s|nineteen sixties}: some sources say {1960|nineteen sixty}, others {1961|nineteen sixty-one}. The jar is named after the place where it was found, and today it's kept at the National Museum of Vietnamese History in Hanoi.","card":{"big":"Red River","sub":"Found by chance on its banks in the early 1960s"}},{"tag":"💞 Four couples","q":"Who are the little figures on the rim of the lid?","a":"The four groups at the four corners of the lid are four couples joined in union: the man wears a loincloth with a dagger at his hip, the woman a short skirt. For wet-rice farmers, the fertility of people was bound up with the fertility of the crops. Researchers see this as a symbol of a fertility cult, a prayer for many descendants and a good rice harvest.","s":"Four couples joined in union, a fertility symbol for rice farmers: when people multiply, so do the harvests."},{"tag":"🕊️ Counterclockwise birds","q":"Which way do the birds on the lid fly?","a":"In one decorative band on the lid, eight birds in four pairs fly one after another counterclockwise, and each pair of human figures is set in the gap between the two birds of a pair. On the faces of Đông Sơn bronze drums, flocks of birds and herds of deer move in exactly the same direction.","card":{"big":"8 birds","sub":"Four pairs of birds fly counterclockwise around the lid"}},{"tag":"🚣 Six boats","q":"What story did the widest band around the middle of the jar, now faded, once tell?","a":"The story of six boats, one behind another, each carrying six or seven people in costume, wearing feathered headdresses, each in a different pose. Around them are flying birds, fish, water birds and crocodiles. The carving has faded now, but the decoration on this jar is considered just as fine as on the most beautiful Đông Sơn bronze drums.","s":"Six boats carrying people in feathered headdresses, surrounded by flying birds, fish and crocodiles. The carving has now faded."},{"tag":"〰️ Geometric patterns","q":"What are the bands of patterns running around the jar's shoulder for?","a":"They divide the jar's body into tiers, like the ruled lines on a page. They're the familiar geometric motifs of Đông Sơn bronzes: rows of small triangles, circles linked by tangent lines, zigzags woven into diamonds, rows of short straight strokes. Thanks to them, the bands of people, boats and birds stand out clearly, with a sense of rhythm."},{"tag":"🌾 Seed rice, then ashes","q":"What did the Đông Sơn people keep in this big lidded jar?","a":"Some say this jar belonged to a chief: at first it held seed rice, and later, the chief's own ashes. A museum in Paris sees Đông Sơn jars as precious objects, used in fertility rites and then buried with the dead as a sign of status.","s":"Some believe it belonged to a chief: first it stored seed rice, and later it held the chief's own ashes."}]},"tuong-cong-nhau-thoi-khen":{"title":"Statue of Two People Piggybacking and Playing the Khèn","artist":"People of the Đông Sơn culture","date":"About the 3rd–2nd century BCE (about 2,000–2,500 years ago)","medium":"Bronze, cast in one piece in a clay mold","where":"National Museum of Vietnamese History, 1 Tràng Tiền, Hanoi","place":{"name":"National Museum of Vietnamese History","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["Đông Sơn","khèn","bronze statue","national treasure"],"hook":"One carries, the other plays the khèn, a bamboo mouth organ, all in a piece of bronze less than ten centimeters tall. The secret of its balance lies in a tiny detail at the statue's feet.","intro":"Statue of Two People Piggybacking and Playing the Khèn: bronze, Đông Sơn culture, some two thousand to two and a half thousand years old, now in the National Museum of Vietnamese History in Hanoi. It has been a National Treasure since {2012|twenty twelve}.","openers":["Look down at this statue's feet: a small flap of cloth is doing the job of a leg.","The one on top is playing an instrument still heard in Vietnam's mountains and forests today. Can you guess it?","Stop and look at the open spaces between the arms and the bodies: the whole statue was cast in a single piece."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours gazing at one tiny bronze statue…","end":"Two people, one khèn, one dance, all wrapped up in eight and a half centimeters of bronze. Now you understand why people can gaze at this tiny statue for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Gary Todd (CC0) · National Museum of Vietnamese History","lic":"CC0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"📏 Just 8.5 cm","q":"How big is this statue?","a":"Just eight and a half centimeters tall and nine and a half wide, small enough to sit in your palm. And yet, some two thousand to two and a half thousand years ago, a Đông Sơn craftsman managed to cast two people and a khèn, with eyes, noses and mouths clearly defined.","s":"Only eight and a half centimeters tall, palm-sized, and cast some two thousand years ago.","card":{"big":"8.5 cm","sub":"8.5 cm tall, 9.5 cm wide: it fits in your palm"}},{"tag":"🎶 The khèn player","q":"What is the one riding on the back doing?","a":"Playing the khèn, lips to the instrument. One arm hugs the carrier, the other holds the khèn. Khèn players also appear on the faces of Đông Sơn bronze drums, and to this day, the sound of the khèn still rings out among many of Vietnam's ethnic minorities.","s":"Playing the khèn, one arm around the carrier: the music of the ancient Vietnamese, still heard in the mountains today."},{"tag":"💃 The dancing carrier","q":"And the carrier: standing still, or moving?","a":"Both arms reach back to hold the rider tight, legs spread wide as if bouncing to the sound of the khèn. Associate Professor Trịnh Sinh describes one leg lifted higher than the other, as if keeping time with a dance, and sees this as a musical celebration of people long ago, people who loved to dance and play music at the same time.","s":"Both arms hold the rider tight, and the legs seem to bounce to the khèn: music and dance become one."},{"tag":"🦶 The third leg","q":"Two people stacked up, and the one below is bouncing, so why doesn't the statue topple over?","a":"Look down: besides the two feet, there's a small bronze bar touching the ground in the middle. According to Associate Professor Trịnh Sinh, it's the flap of the carrier's loincloth, hanging down to the ground to form a third leg. Three points of support keep the whole bronze steady: a small but clever trick of the Đông Sơn casters.","s":"The loincloth flap reaches the ground, a third leg that keeps the whole statue standing."},{"tag":"👂 Big earrings","q":"Can you see the big round earring beside the carrier's head?","a":"According to Associate Professor Trịnh Sinh, both wear large earrings and round hair buns, and the carrier also has a cloth wound around the head. Eyes, noses and mouths are cast in clear detail. Small details like these are precious clues to how the Đông Sơn people dressed and adorned themselves."},{"tag":"🔥 Openwork casting","q":"Can you see the open spaces passing between the arms and bodies?","a":"That's an openwork form with many angles, which demands complex mold-making. The statue was cast in one piece in a clay mold, not put together from several parts. It has been described as the liveliest of all Đông Sơn sculptures in the round."},{"tag":"🧑‍🤝‍🧑 Who are they?","q":"How are these two related?","a":"There's no definite answer yet. The English translation of the recognition decision and some of the museum's pages call them a couple. But Associate Professor Trịnh Sinh and many Vietnamese-language articles describe them as two men. What anyone can see is that the two of them are making merry together."},{"tag":"🔩 Part of an object?","q":"What was this little statue for?","a":"The museum suggests it may once have been attached to the lid or handle of some everyday object. Sculpture in the round is rare in Đông Sơn culture, and according to Associate Professor Trịnh Sinh, surveys of hundreds of sites and graves have yet to turn up a second statue like this one."},{"tag":"🗺️ Two backstories","q":"Where was the statue found?","a":"In Thanh Hóa, but its record comes in two versions. Some of the museum's pages say it lay in a brick tomb at Lạch Trường, in Hoằng Hóa, excavated by the Swedish archaeologist Olov Janse, and came to the museum in {1935|nineteen thirty-five}. Associate Professor Trịnh Sinh, however, writes that the statue was found by a Frenchman named {Pajot|Pah-zho} at the Đông Sơn site.","card":{"big":"Thanh Hóa","sub":"Lạch Trường brick tomb or the Đông Sơn site? Sources differ"}}]},"dai-tho-tra-kieu":{"title":"The Trà Kiệu Altar","artist":"Champa artisans (anonymous)","date":"About the 7th century (linked to the stele of King Prakāśadharma, c. 657–687); some date it to the 10th century","medium":"Sandstone","where":"Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture (Trà Kiệu gallery, inventory no. BTC 95)","place":{"name":"Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture","prov":"Đà Nẵng"},"tags":["Champa","Ramayana","relief sculpture","national treasure"],"hook":"A square block of stone, its four sides carved with more than sixty figures, telling a story that runs all the way around. For nearly a hundred years, scholars have been arguing: whose story is it?","intro":"The Trà Kiệu Altar, in sandstone, from Champa, an ancient kingdom of central Vietnam, thought to date from around the seventh century. It's now in the Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture, a National Treasure since {2012|twenty twelve}.","openers":["Who are the more than sixty figures carved around this block of stone? Scholars have been arguing for nearly a century.","Stop and look at the corners of the block: lions standing upright, holding up the whole altar with their front paws.","Why does one entire side of this altar show nothing but dancing celestial maidens?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours circling a single Cham altar…","end":"One square block of stone, four sides, more than sixty figures and at least four different readings. Now you understand why people can walk around the Trà Kiệu Altar for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Noel Hidalgo Tan (CC BY-SA 4.0) · Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture","lic":"CC0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🦁 Lions holding it up","q":"What's the lion on this corner doing?","a":"Standing upright, holding up with its front paws a square block of stone nearly two meters on each side, and all four corners have a lion just like it. In {1901|nineteen oh one}, the altar was brought to Đà Nẵng from Trà Kiệu, believed to be the Champa capital Simhapura, a name that means City of Lions.","s":"Four upright lions at the corners hold up the whole stone block, nearly two meters a side, with their front paws."},{"tag":"❓ Whose story?","q":"Who's this crowd standing shoulder to shoulder on this side?","a":"That's a riddle nearly a century old. In {1929|nineteen twenty-nine}, one scholar read it as the founding legend of Funan, an ancient kingdom. In {1931|nineteen thirty-one}, {George Cœdès|George Saydess} saw the story of the god Krishna. Then researcher Trần Kỳ Phương recognized the Indian epic Ramayana: Prince Rama breaking a divine bow to win the hand of Princess Sita, the reading most widely accepted today.","s":"Funan, Krishna or Rama? The most widely accepted reading: Prince Rama breaks a divine bow to marry Princess Sita."},{"tag":"📜 A chapter per side","q":"In what order does the story run around the altar?","a":"In the Ramayana reading, each side is a chapter: Rama breaks the divine bow; King Janaka's envoys bring the good news to King Dasaratha, Rama's father; then the king travels to Mithila for the wedding, where King Janaka gives Sita to Rama in marriage. The chapters follow one another around the stone like a scroll."},{"tag":"💃 Eleven Apsaras","q":"Why does one whole side of the altar show nothing but dancing girls?","a":"They're eleven Apsaras, celestial maidens, dancing and singing to celebrate the wedding. According to some studies, the side with the bow also shows two Gandharvas, celestial performers, so the story closes in a circle: it begins with the bow and ends with the dance.","s":"Eleven Apsaras, celestial maidens, dance and sing for the wedding, closing the story that runs around the altar."},{"tag":"🔱 Linga and yoni","q":"And what's the round piece on top of the altar?","a":"That's a linga and yoni, the symbol of the god Shiva, on two round tiers carved with lotus petals. For the Cham, an altar links the gods with the temple, and heaven with earth. But according to some sources, this set was only placed here after the altar came to Đà Nẵng, and the current linga is a replica."},{"tag":"💎 The Trà Kiệu style","q":"Look closely at one figure: what are they wearing, and what jewelry?","a":"A big bun of hair at the nape, a bare chest, layer upon layer of beads around the neck and arms, and a broad beaded belt. These clothes and ornaments, along with the beaded border running along the edge of the relief, are the telltale signs of the Trà Kiệu style in Cham sculpture."},{"tag":"📅 How old is it?","q":"How old is this altar?","a":"No one can say for sure. Trần Kỳ Phương links the altar to a stele of King Prakashadharma found at Trà Kiệu, from around {657 to 687|six fifty-seven to six eighty-seven}, which mentions a temple to Valmiki, regarded as the author of the Ramayana. Many others, going by style, place it in the tenth century. The museum says the seventh to eighth century, with a question mark.","s":"The seventh century, going by a stele, or the tenth, going by style? It's still not settled.","card":{"big":"VII or X?","sub":"7th or 10th century? Scholars still disagree"}},{"tag":"🪷 A 2025 hypothesis","q":"Is the riddle solved yet?","a":"Not yet. In late {2025|twenty twenty-five}, architect Lê Trí Công put forward a new hypothesis: the four sides tell the life of the Buddha, from Prince Siddhartha's archery contest to his return to his homeland. It's only one person's view so far, but it shows how this altar still keeps people hooked on cracking its code.","card":{"big":"2025","sub":"A new hypothesis: the life of the Buddha?"}}]},"dai-tho-my-son-e1":{"title":"The Mỹ Sơn E1 Altar","artist":"Champa artisans (anonymous)","date":"About the 7th–8th century (some sources say the second half of the 7th century)","medium":"Sandstone, assembled from 16 blocks (14 survive)","where":"Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture (Mỹ Sơn gallery); originally in tower E1, Mỹ Sơn Sanctuary","place":{"name":"Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture","prov":"Đà Nẵng"},"tags":["Champa","Mỹ Sơn","hermits","national treasure"],"hook":"This is the only Champa altar found so far that's carved like a miniature temple tower, with steps, arched doorways and door pillars. And in each little doorway, someone is playing music, or a hermit is reading scripture and preaching in the forest.","intro":"The {Mỹ Sơn E1|Mỹ Sơn E one} Altar, in sandstone, from the ancient kingdom of Champa, around the seventh to eighth century. It was excavated at the Mỹ Sơn Sanctuary in the early twentieth century and is now in the Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture.","openers":["Try to find the person sitting cross-legged in the doorway to the right of the steps: they're holding a harp.","Stop and look at the steps: three people are lifting a long scarf, as if raising the whole altar toward the sky.","Why would an altar have steps, arched doorways and door pillars, like a temple tower?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at one ancient altar…","end":"A sacred mountain in miniature, where musicians play, hermits read scripture, and the faithful kneel to listen. Now you understand why people can gaze at the {Mỹ Sơn E1|Mỹ Sơn E one} Altar for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · Đà Nẵng Museum of Cham Sculpture","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: DoktorMax (public domain)","lic":"Public domain"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🏯 A miniature tower","q":"Why would an altar have steps, arched doorways and door pillars, like a temple tower?","a":"This is the only altar found so far that reproduces the architecture of a Cham temple tower in detail. In Hindu belief, it is the sacred mountain where the god Shiva dwells: some sources call it Mount Meru, others Mount Kailasa, where hermits practice their devotions in forests and caves.","s":"The only altar that reproduces a Cham temple tower: an image of the sacred mountain where the god Shiva dwells."},{"tag":"🧣 The scarf dance","q":"What are the people on the steps lifting up?","a":"A sacred scarf. On the lower step, three people lift it together, the one in the middle raising a hand as if holding up the whole altar. On the upper step, a figure with their back to us kneels on one knee, lifting the scarf high. Flanking the balustrade are makaras, mythical water monsters guarding the way into sacred ground.","s":"Dancers lift a sacred scarf as an offering to the gods, flanked by makara water monsters guarding the entrance."},{"tag":"🪜 A temple's base?","q":"Why would an altar need steps to climb up?","a":"The altar was assembled from sixteen sandstone blocks, of which fourteen survive. Researcher Trần Kỳ Phương has an intriguing hypothesis: at first, this may have been the base of a small wooden temple, its shrine open to the sky. Later, the temple was rebuilt on a larger scale, and the old base was turned into an altar pedestal inside it."},{"tag":"🎵 The harp player","q":"What's the cross-legged figure in the doorway right of the steps holding?","a":"A curved harp with a boat-shaped sound box. The carver didn't show the strings, but the pose alone is enough to evoke the music. Opposite, on the left, another figure plays a transverse flute. According to researcher {Patrick Kersalé|Patrick Kersalay}, the altar also shows a one-string instrument, another flute and a set of three drums.","s":"A curved harp with a boat-shaped sound box; opposite, on the left, another figure plays a transverse flute."},{"tag":"📖 A hermit reading","q":"What is the long-bearded hermit in the doorway beside the steps doing?","a":"Reading a sacred text. Researcher Mya Chau sees this as a sign that knowledge from ancient texts circulated in Champa. Right above him is the head of a makara from the balustrade. Notice: ascetic hermits grow long beards, while Brahmin priests are clean-shaven and wear strings of beads."},{"tag":"🙏 Listening to a sermon","q":"Who is the person kneeling there, palms joined at the chest?","a":"A devotee listening to the hermit preach. Every doorway around the altar is a scene of daily life in the forests and mountains: making music, talking, meditating. Some sources also mention scenes of preparing medicine, massage, and even animals dancing to the music. This is the only Champa altar that tells the life of these holy men in such detail."},{"tag":"🔱 The lost linga","q":"What once stood on top of the altar?","a":"A linga, the symbol of the god Shiva, now lost. In {2012|twenty twelve}, after heavy rain, a mukhalinga, a linga bearing the face of the god, more than {1.2 meters|one point two meters} tall, was found nearby. Many experts believe it was most likely once worshipped on this very altar.","card":{"big":"Lost linga","sub":"In 2012, a mukhalinga over 1.2 m tall was found nearby"}},{"tag":"🎨 The E1 style","q":"Why is an entire art style named after this temple?","a":"Scholars see the {Mỹ Sơn E1|Mỹ Sơn E one} style, from around the seventh to eighth century, as the founding style of Champa sculpture, and this altar as its most famous work. The patterns on the altar are also close to the Mon art of Dvaravati, in Thailand, from the same period.","s":"The founding style of Champa sculpture is named after temple {E1|E one}, and this altar is its most famous work.","card":{"big":"Mỹ Sơn E1","sub":"A whole art style named after this temple"}}]},"a-di-da-phat-tich":{"title":"The Amitabha Buddha of Phật Tích Pagoda","artist":"Lý-dynasty artisan (anonymous)","date":"Lý dynasty, linked to a stele inscription of 1057 (reign of Emperor Lý Thánh Tông)","medium":"A single block of blue-gray stone; once covered in red lacquer and gilding","where":"Phật Tích Pagoda (Vạn Phúc Temple), Lạn Kha Mountain, Tiên Du, Bắc Ninh","place":{"name":"Phật Tích Pagoda","prov":"Bắc Ninh"},"tags":["Buddhist sculpture","Lý dynasty","stone carving","national treasure","Amitabha"],"hook":"This Buddha rests his left hand on top of his right, the opposite of the usual way, and to this day no one agrees on which Buddha he is. The original, nearly a thousand years old, was even once used for target practice.","intro":"The Amitabha Buddha of Phật Tích Pagoda in Bắc Ninh, carved from a single block of blue-gray stone in the Lý dynasty and linked to a stele inscription from {1057|ten fifty-seven}; a National Treasure since {2012|twenty twelve}. The images in this video show reproductions on display at the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts and the Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of History.","openers":["Stop and look at the hands resting in the statue's lap: the left hand lies on top of the right.","Try to find the dragons coiling inside the lotus petals beneath the Buddha.","Why are this Buddha's eyes only half closed, gazing down?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours sitting before a single stone Buddha…","end":"One block of blue-gray stone, nearly a thousand years, a head once broken off, and a patched robe. Now you understand why people can sit for hours before the Buddha of Phật Tích Pagoda.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · reproduction at the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Daderot (CC0) · reproduction at the Vietnam National Museum of Fine Arts","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Phương Huy (CC BY-SA 4.0) · reproduction at the Hồ Chí Minh City Museum of History","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🪨 One block of stone","q":"How many blocks of stone was the original statue carved from?","a":"Just one, a single block of blue-gray stone. The original statue is about {1.86 meters|one meter eighty-six} tall, nearly {2.7 meters|two point seven meters} with its pedestal. A stele inscription from {1057|ten fifty-seven} records that Emperor Lý Thánh Tông had the pagoda built and the statue placed in a tower on Lạn Kha Mountain. One art researcher calls it the oldest and most accomplished Amitabha statue of the Lý dynasty.","s":"One block of blue-gray stone, nearly {2.7 meters|two point seven meters} tall with its pedestal, tied to a {1057|ten fifty-seven} stele from Emperor Lý Thánh Tông's reign."},{"tag":"😌 Half-closed eyes","q":"Why are the Buddha's eyes only half open?","a":"An oval face, half-closed eyes looking down, three folds on the neck, long earlobes. Eyes half shut and half open are the gaze of meditation: perfectly still within, yet still watching over everything around, as if ready to save all living beings."},{"tag":"🌀 Curls and ushnisha","q":"What are the little round bumps covering the statue's head?","a":"They're tight curls of hair, and the mound rising on top of the head is called the ushnisha. Curled hair, a tall neck and long earlobes are all among the noble marks of a Buddha. The tall neck and the tall, sturdy build in particular have been linked by one Buddhist studies scholar to Chinese Buddha statues of the Six Dynasties and the Sui and Tang periods."},{"tag":"👘 A robe thin as silk","q":"Why does a robe made of stone look as thin as cloth?","a":"The Buddha wears two layers of robes that cling to the body like freshly wet cloth, the folds flowing softly from the shoulders down into the lap and over both legs. This wet-drapery style makes the heavy stone feel light. Some say the hem draped over the legs of the original statue was once ground flat, which is why it looks cut off."},{"tag":"🤲 Left hand on top","q":"Did you notice which hand is on top?","a":"Both hands lie palm up, one on the other in the lap: the gesture of meditation. But here the left hand rests on top of the right, the opposite of what you usually see. The statue holds another riddle too: is this Amitabha, Shakyamuni, or {Vairocana|Vairochana}? Scholars still disagree, and Amitabha remains the most familiar name.","s":"The left hand rests on top of the right, the opposite of the usual way. Scholars are even still debating whether this is Amitabha or Shakyamuni."},{"tag":"🐉 Dragons in the lotus","q":"What's carved on the lotus petals beneath the Buddha?","a":"Coiling dragons with round bodies, winding as softly as snakes: the classic Lý-dynasty dragon. The lotus throne is a flattened half-sphere, with petals turned both down and up. The dragon was a symbol of emperors and kings, so finding it inside the lotus petals beneath the Buddha is a detail that makes many people curious.","s":"Round-bodied dragons winding like snakes, the classic Lý-dynasty dragon, tucked right inside the lotus petals beneath the Buddha."},{"tag":"🌊 A pedestal of waves","q":"Below the lotus throne, what else does the stone pedestal tell us?","a":"On the reproduction displayed in Hồ Chí Minh City, you can clearly see the octagonal pedestal with its several tiers: the upper tiers are carved with dragons twisting among clouds and flames, and the lowest ones with layers of waves. Researchers date the patterns on the pedestal to the Lý dynasty, though some believe the statue and the pedestal may not be from quite the same period."},{"tag":"✨ Once gilded","q":"What color was the original statue long ago?","a":"Gold. The Lý-dynasty stele mentions a gilded statue, and in the French colonial period, the French scholar {Bezacier|Bay-zah-syay} still saw it lacquered red and gilded, set in the most honored place in the pagoda. Through war and the passing years, that gold has disappeared, revealing the blue-gray stone of the original statue you see today.","card":{"big":"Gilded","sub":"The original was once lacquered red and gilded; today the blue-gray stone shows"}},{"tag":"🩹 A patched robe","q":"Why does the original statue at Phật Tích Pagoda wear a patched robe?","a":"Because of bullet marks. During the resistance war against the French, the pagoda was burned and the original statue was used for target practice, its head and neck broken off. An old man secretly carried the head home and hid it, only returning it when peace came, so it could be put back as before. The lotus-leaf robe still bears its patches: the abbot jokes that the statue isn't broken, it just has to wear a patched robe.","s":"Bullet marks from the war against the French: shots broke off the original's head, an old man hid it, and returned it in peacetime to be put back."}]},"cuu-dinh-hue":{"title":"The Nine Dynastic Urns","artist":"Bronze casters of the Huế capital, by order of Emperor Minh Mạng","date":"1835–1837 (reign of Emperor Minh Mạng)","medium":"Bronze; relief casting combined with engraving","where":"Courtyard of the Thế Miếu temple, in front of the Hiển Lâm Pavilion, Huế Imperial City","place":{"name":"Thế Miếu, Huế Imperial City","prov":"Huế"},"tags":["Nguyễn dynasty","Huế","bronze casting","national treasure","Memory of the World"],"hook":"Nine bronze urns, nine names. Seven of the names match the posthumous titles of seven Nguyễn emperors, but two of the urns, for nearly two hundred years now, have never been matched to any emperor.","intro":"The Nine Dynastic Urns, cast in bronze under Emperor Minh Mạng from {1835|eighteen thirty-five} to {1837|eighteen thirty-seven}, stand in front of Thế Miếu, the temple of the Nguyễn emperors, inside Huế's Imperial City. A National Treasure since {2012|twenty twelve}.","openers":["Try to find the two Chinese characters on the body of the nearest urn: they match an emperor's posthumous title.","Why is there a jackfruit tree bearing fruit on Emperor Gia Long's urn?","Stop and look at the tiger on the largest urn: it shares a row with a cannon."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours standing before nine bronze urns…","end":"Nine urns, one hundred fifty-three reliefs: the sky, mountains and rivers, the sea, plants, animals and the riches of an entire country, cast in bronze. Now you understand why people can stand for hours before the Nine Dynastic Urns.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Dennis G. Jarvis (CC BY-SA 2.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"by":"Photo: Phương Huy (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Phương Huy (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Phương Huy (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🔤 Nine names","q":"What do the two Chinese characters on the nearest urn say?","a":"Read from right to left, the characters say Nhân đỉnh, the Nhân urn, which belongs to Emperor Minh Mạng. The nine urns bear nine names: Cao, Nhân, Chương, Anh, Nghị, Thuần, Tuyên, Dụ, Huyền. The first seven match the posthumous titles of seven emperors, from Gia Long to Khải Định, and according to some sources, later titles were chosen in the order of the urn names. The Dụ and Huyền urns still have no owner.","s":"Nhân đỉnh, the Nhân urn, which belongs to Emperor Minh Mạng. Seven urns match the posthumous titles of seven emperors, from Gia Long to Khải Định. The Dụ and Huyền urns still have no owner."},{"tag":"👑 The Cao urn","q":"This urn standing right in the middle: which emperor is it for?","a":"Emperor Gia Long, founder of the Nguyễn dynasty. The two Chinese characters on it read Cao đỉnh, the Cao urn. It's the largest of the set, about two and a half meters tall and weighing roughly {2.6 tons|two point six tons}. It stands directly in line with Gia Long's altar inside Thế Miếu, and it's the only one set about three meters ahead of the other eight, to honor the dynasty's founder.","s":"Emperor Gia Long, founder of the Nguyễn dynasty. The Cao urn is the largest of the set, weighing about {2.6 tons|two point six tons}, and the only one set forward from the rest."},{"tag":"🔢 153 or 162?","q":"How many reliefs are there on the nine urns in all?","a":"Each urn has eighteen panels: seventeen with images and one bearing the urn's name. That's why some count one hundred fifty-three images, while others say one hundred sixty-two, counting the nine name panels too. Each relief is a complete work in itself, cast in one piece with the body of the urn.","s":"Each urn has seventeen reliefs and one name panel: one hundred fifty-three images, or one hundred sixty-two panels if you count the names.","card":{"big":"153 + 9","sub":"153 reliefs and 9 name panels: 162 cast panels"}},{"tag":"📚 Three rows of images","q":"Why are the reliefs arranged in three rows?","a":"Each row holds one group of subjects. The middle row is heaven and earth: the sun, stars, mountains, rivers, the sea, and the name panel right in front of you. The top and bottom rows show animals, plants, local products, boats and weapons, like that dragon coiled in the clouds. Each panel is labeled in Chinese characters, because Emperor Minh Mạng ordered every name and place of origin to be clearly engraved."},{"tag":"🏞️ Vĩnh Tế Canal","q":"What name do these three Chinese characters, set between mountains and waves, spell out?","a":"Vĩnh Tế hà: the Vĩnh Tế Canal. On the bronze panel, the canal runs straight between a band of mountains above and layers of waves below. This panel sits in the middle row of the Cao urn, along with the sun, the East Sea, the Bến Nghé River and Thiên Tôn Mountain. The sky is shared out across all nine urns: the moon on the Nhân urn, five stars on the Chương urn, the Big Dipper on the Anh urn."},{"tag":"🌳 The emperor's jackfruit","q":"On Emperor Gia Long's urn, what tree is bearing fruit right on its trunk?","a":"A jackfruit tree: the three Chinese characters beside it read ba la mật. The same row has a dragon, a rice plant, scallions, crape myrtle flowers and a pheasant: the dragon of the imperial court stands level with a jackfruit tree and garden scallions. The urns blend courtly and folk themes, and about ninety of the images are plants and animals typical of Vietnam.","s":"A jackfruit tree, written ba la mật in Chinese characters. A dragon shares its row: the court's dragon stands level with a jackfruit tree and garden scallions."},{"tag":"🐅 Tiger and cannon","q":"Why does this tiger share a row with a cannon?","a":"The tiger sits among the plants, with the Chinese character Hổ, tiger, carved in the top corner. It's in the bottom row of the Cao urn, with ironwood, a sailing ship, a cannon, agarwood and a softshell turtle: forests and mountains, rivers and seas, precious wood, aromatics and military might, all on a single urn. The Huế Monuments Conservation Centre compares the urns to an encyclopedia in pictures."},{"tag":"🔥 Sixty furnaces","q":"How do you cast an urn weighing more than two tons?","a":"Huế's casters worked by hand, and each urn had its own clay mold, broken after casting, so none could ever be reproduced. According to a museum expert in Huế, each urn needed about sixty furnaces to melt the bronze, each melting just thirty to forty kilos. A small line of characters on the neck of the Nhân urn records its weight: more than four thousand cân, an old Vietnamese unit."},{"tag":"🏛️ UNESCO 2024","q":"What did UNESCO inscribe in {2024|twenty twenty-four}?","a":"In {2024|twenty twenty-four}, in the capital of Mongolia, the relief castings on the nine bronze urns of Huế's Imperial Palace were added to UNESCO's Memory of the World Register for Asia and the Pacific, with the agreement of all twenty-three member countries. What was inscribed is the reliefs themselves, as a set of documents in bronze.","card":{"big":"2024","sub":"UNESCO: Memory of the World, Asia-Pacific"}}]},"nha-nhac-cung-dinh-hue":{"title":"Nhã nhạc: Huế Court Music","artist":"Court musicians of the Nguyễn dynasty and generations of Huế artists (today the Huế Royal Traditional Arts Theatre)","date":"From the 15th century (Lê dynasty) to the mid-20th century; most complete under the Nguyễn dynasty (1802–1945)","medium":"Ceremonial court music: the Đại nhạc ensemble (drums, oboes) and the Tiểu nhạc ensemble (strings, flutes, clappers), with song and dance","where":"Huế: the Imperial City (Duyệt Thị Đường royal theater), the Nam Giao Esplanade, the dynastic temples; performed today by the Huế Royal Traditional Arts Theatre","place":{"name":"Huế Imperial City, Duyệt Thị Đường royal theater","prov":"Huế"},"tags":["nhã nhạc","court music","Nguyễn dynasty","Huế","UNESCO"],"hook":"These musicians in red march through the night with drums and oboes, and not a single string instrument in sight. At the Huế court, that was no accident: every ceremony had its own kind of music.","intro":"Nhã nhạc, the court music of Huế: the ceremonial music of the royal court, at its most complete under the Nguyễn dynasty. UNESCO proclaimed it a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in {2003|two thousand three}, and added it to the Representative List in {2008|two thousand eight}.","openers":["Try to find an oboe in this ensemble with a round wooden bell shaped like a gourd.","Stop and look at these musicians in red: there are drums, there are oboes, but not a single string instrument.","There's a photo from {1937|nineteen thirty-seven} of the Huế court orchestra, taken while the Nguyễn dynasty still reigned. What instruments are they holding?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours listening to the court music of Huế…","end":"Drums and oboes for grand rites, strings and flutes for banquets, a different music for every ceremony, and even instruments brought back after they had been lost. Now you understand why people can listen to the court music of Huế for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Lưu Ly (public domain) · Huế Festival 2008","lic":"Public domain"},{"by":"Photo: Badagnani (CC BY 3.0)","lic":"CC BY 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: anonymous (CC0) · archival photo taken in 1937","lic":"CC0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🥁 Drums and oboes","q":"Why does this ensemble carry drums and oboes, but not a single string instrument?","a":"Because nhã nhạc has two main ensembles. Đại nhạc, the great ensemble, also called cổ xuy, is the ensemble of drums and oboes, heard at grand rites such as the Nam Giao sacrifice, temple ceremonies and grand court audiences. Strings and flutes belong to the other, Tiểu nhạc, the small ensemble: softer, for banquets, the Lunar New Year or welcoming guests.","s":"Drums and oboes form the Đại nhạc ensemble, for grand rites; strings and flutes belong to the softer Tiểu nhạc, for banquets and guests."},{"tag":"🎺 The kèn bầu","q":"What's this oboe called, with its bell bulging round like a gourd?","a":"The kèn bầu, the gourd oboe. Its body is hardwood with seven finger holes, and its bell is carved from jackfruit wood in the shape of a gourd; some say that at first, people used an actual dried gourd. Unlike its cousins in China or Korea, the kèn bầu has a detachable bell. Together with the drums, these oboes are the backbone of the Đại nhạc ensemble.","s":"The kèn bầu: a wooden body with seven finger holes, a detachable gourd-shaped bell of jackfruit wood, and the backbone of the Đại nhạc."},{"tag":"🎻 Tiểu nhạc, 1937","q":"Which ensemble is in this black-and-white photo?","a":"Taken in {1937|nineteen thirty-seven}, it shows the Huế court's Tiểu nhạc ensemble performing at Diên Thọ Palace, with bamboo flutes, the bowed đàn nhị fiddle and the two-stringed đàn nguyệt lute. This silk-and-bamboo ensemble also had the four-stringed tỳ bà, the three-stringed đàn tam covered in snakeskin, plus phách tiền clappers and a trống bản drum to keep time.","s":"A {1937|nineteen thirty-seven} photo: the Huế court's Tiểu nhạc ensemble with bamboo flute, đàn nhị fiddle and đàn nguyệt moon lute, an ensemble of silk and bamboo."},{"tag":"📜 Music for every rite","q":"Is nhã nhạc one and the same music for every ceremony?","a":"No. There was music for the sacrifice to Heaven and Earth at the Nam Giao Esplanade, temple music for the dynastic shrines, music for rites honoring deities such as the Xã Tắc altar of soil and grain, music for grand and for regular court audiences, banquet music for great feasts, and music for use inside the palace.","card":{"big":"7 kinds","sub":"Every court ceremony had its own music"}},{"tag":"👘 Red with gold trim","q":"Red robes trimmed with gold, black turbans: did court musicians always dress like this?","a":"Not exactly. In Huế, the musicians sometimes wear blue, sometimes red trimmed with gold, and researchers have also asked which one is right. According to researcher Phan Thuận Thảo, the Nguyễn court had many kinds of costume for its musicians, differing between the Tiểu nhạc and Đại nhạc ensembles and by occasion: grand rites, ordinary rites or funerals."},{"tag":"🔔 Bells and stone chimes","q":"Were any court instruments ever completely lost?","a":"Yes. The biên chung is a set of twelve bronze bells, and the biên khánh twelve stone chimes shaped like an upside-down L, both hung on wooden frames lacquered red and gilded. Under the Nguyễn, they sounded only at grand rites such as grand court audiences and the Nam Giao and Xã Tắc sacrifices. By the late twentieth century, both had been lost, and then they were recreated together with Korean experts.","card":{"big":"12 + 12","sub":"Bronze bells and stone chimes, for grand rites only"}},{"tag":"🕯️ Keeping the flame alive","q":"With no more royal court, who kept nhã nhạc alive?","a":"When the monarchy came to an end and war dragged on, nhã nhạc lost its place, and only a handful of former court musicians kept the craft going. Learning from them, Huế artists collected and revived nearly a hundred pieces, both ritual music and court dances, and opened classes to pass them on to the young."},{"tag":"🏆 Masterpiece, 2003","q":"When did the world first honor nhã nhạc?","a":"On {November 7, 2003|November seventh, two thousand three}, UNESCO proclaimed nhã nhạc a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity. It was Vietnam's first intangible heritage to be recognized by UNESCO. In {2008|two thousand eight}, it was inscribed on the Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity.","s":"On {November 7, 2003|November seventh, two thousand three}, nhã nhạc became Vietnam's first intangible heritage to be honored by UNESCO.","card":{"big":"2003","sub":"November 7: Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage"}}]},"cong-chieng-tay-nguyen":{"title":"The Space of Gong Culture in the Central Highlands","artist":"The ethnic communities of the Central Highlands: Ba Na, Xơ Đăng, Gia Rai, Ê Đê, Mnông, Cơ Ho, Mạ, Chu Ru…","date":"A long-standing tradition, still alive in village rituals and festivals; UNESCO: Masterpiece 2005, Representative List 2008","medium":"Gong music tied to rituals, festivals and xoang circle dances; brass gongs","where":"Highland villages in Gia Lai, Đắk Lắk, Lâm Đồng and Quảng Ngãi (province names after the 2025 merger; including the former Kon Tum and Đắk Nông)","place":{"name":"Akô Dhông village (Ê Đê), Buôn Ma Thuột","prov":"Đắk Lắk"},"tags":["gong culture","Central Highlands","festival","UNESCO"],"hook":"Each man here holds just one gong, and no one can play a melody on his own. And according to the peoples of the Central Highlands, a spirit lives inside every single gong.","intro":"The Space of Gong Culture in Vietnam's Central Highlands, the heritage of peoples such as the Ba Na, Gia Rai, Ê Đê, Mnông and Cơ Ho. UNESCO proclaimed it a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity in {2005|two thousand five}, and added it to the Representative List in {2008|two thousand eight}.","openers":["Look at the men in the circle: each holds just one gong and strikes just one note.","Stop and look at which way the whole group is moving: they walk counterclockwise around the nêu pole.","Look at the center of this big gong: a round knob sticks out. Do you know the difference between a cồng and a chiêng?"],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours listening to a gong ensemble…","end":"A gong ensemble only becomes music when the whole village plays together, and every gong is a soul cherished through the generations. Now you understand why people can listen to a gong ensemble for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Levanchaugl1981 (CC0) · H'way village gong ensemble","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Levanchaugl1981 (CC0) · H'way village gong ensemble","lic":"CC0"},{"by":"Photo: Trúc Đồng (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🎵 One note each","q":"Why does each person hold just one gong?","a":"Because each gong gives only one pitch, and each player strikes just one note, in a rhythmic pattern of their own. The melody only appears when everyone plays together, their notes interlocking. In many Southeast Asian countries, gongs are arranged in sets for a single player, but in the Central Highlands it's still one gong per person.","s":"Each person strikes just one note, and the melody only emerges when the whole ensemble plays together, interlocking."},{"tag":"🌀 Counterclockwise","q":"Which way is the whole group moving?","a":"Counterclockwise. At many festivals, people play the gongs while walking in a circle around the nêu pole, with the dancers following behind. According to some sources, this direction is linked to the path of the sun and to how the Highland peoples see the balance between yin and yang."},{"tag":"🎋 The nêu pole","q":"What's that tall pole with a curved top and red-and-white tassels?","a":"It's the nêu pole, the sacred axis of the festival, where the spirits come down and go back up; the taller the pole, the more sacred it is. The Ba Na raise poles ten to twenty meters high, beside the rông communal house. In sacrificial rites, the buffalo is tied to the pole; at some ceremonies, jars of rượu cần, rice wine sipped through straws, are tied around it too, to welcome guests."},{"tag":"💃 Who plays, who dances","q":"Why do the men play the gongs while the women dance?","a":"This line of women in black skirts trimmed with red is dancing to the beat of the gongs. At many festivals, such as those of the Cơ Ho, in the past only men played the gongs while the women danced. But in the southern Central Highlands there are now women's gong teams among the Cơ Ho, Mnông and Chu Ru, their playing described as warmer and gentler, yet still faithful to the old rules."},{"tag":"🔔 Cồng or chiêng","q":"What's the difference between a cồng and a chiêng?","a":"In everyday usage, a cồng is usually large, with a knob rising from its center like this one, while a chiêng is smaller and flat, with no knob. They're made of brass, and each ethnic group has its own way of arranging the ensemble, and its own modes. The Highland peoples don't cast gongs themselves; they buy or trade for them from the Kinh, Vietnam's majority people, and from the Lao and the Cambodians."},{"tag":"👂 The gong tuner","q":"Once a gong is bought, can it be played right away?","a":"Not yet. A gong bought from elsewhere must be retuned by a master to fit the village's scale. The tuner leans in close to listen to each gong, tapping a small hammer on the inside to raise its pitch, or on the outside to lower it. Some places tune gongs so that one person can play a whole piece, but Professor Tô Ngọc Thanh calls that ruining the gongs.","s":"A newly bought gong needs a master who listens up close and taps a hammer on its inside or outside, tuning it to fit the village's scale."},{"tag":"👻 The spirit in the gong","q":"Why do the Highland peoples make offerings to a gong before playing it?","a":"Because they believe a spirit dwells in every gong, and the older the gong, the stronger its spirit. A gong hanging on the wall must receive an offering before it's taken down, and during sacrifices, the animal's blood is often smeared on the gongs. Precious gongs ring out only at major ceremonies, where the sacrifice is usually a buffalo or something greater.","s":"They believe a spirit dwells in every gong, and the older the gong, the stronger its spirit."},{"tag":"🐘 Worth an elephant","q":"In the old days, what was a precious set of gongs worth?","a":"Many precious sets cost thirty or forty buffaloes, and some were traded for a whole elephant. For the Highland peoples, gongs are a family's wealth and standing, like gold for the lowlanders. UNESCO also notes that gongs are a sign of wealth, power and prestige.","s":"Many precious sets cost thirty or forty buffaloes, and some were traded for a whole elephant.","card":{"big":"30–40 buffalo","sub":"The old price of a precious gong set; some cost an elephant"}},{"tag":"🏆 UNESCO 2005","q":"What exactly did UNESCO honor in the gong culture of the Central Highlands?","a":"In {2005|two thousand five}, UNESCO proclaimed the Space of Gong Culture in the Central Highlands a Masterpiece of the Oral and Intangible Heritage of Humanity, and in {2008|two thousand eight} added it to the Representative List. What's honored isn't just the gong, but the whole space it lives in: the villages, the festivals, the rituals and the people who still play.","card":{"big":"2005","sub":"Masterpiece of Humanity; on the Representative List in 2008"}}]},"ca-tru":{"title":"Ca trù Singing","artist":"Ca trù guilds: the đào nương singer, the kép lute player and the quan viên on the praise drum","date":"Present since at least the 15th century; at its height in the early 20th century; gradually revived from the 1990s–2000s","medium":"Sung poetry with accompaniment: the đào nương sings and beats the phách clappers, the kép plucks the đàn đáy lute, the quan viên strikes the praise drum","where":"Northern and north-central provinces (Hanoi, Phú Thọ, Nam Định, Hải Dương, Nghệ An, Hà Tĩnh, Quảng Bình…); many clubs in Hanoi","place":{"name":"Lỗ Khê ca trù village, Đông Anh, home of the Ca Công ancestral temple","prov":"Hanoi"},"tags":["ca trù","đàn đáy","UNESCO","urgent safeguarding"],"hook":"In this singing session, the most discerning listener doesn't clap; they strike a drum. And the singer sings while beating out her own rhythm.","intro":"Ca trù, the sung poetry of northern Vietnam, performed by three people: the đào nương beating her clappers, the kép plucking the đàn đáy lute, and the quan viên on the praise drum. In {2009|two thousand nine}, UNESCO inscribed ca trù on the List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding.","openers":["Look at the singer seated in the middle: her hands rest on a small wooden block, and she beats her own rhythm as she sings.","Try to find the lute with a really long neck and very high frets. It has only three strings, and its name sounds like a riddle.","Stop at the person sitting by the small drum, holding a stick: they're not accompanying the music, they're scoring each line of the song."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours listening to ca trù…","end":"One person singing and beating the clappers, one plucking a lute with no bottom, one giving praise or criticism with a drum. Now you understand why people can listen to a ca trù session for hours.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Michael Coghlan (CC BY-SA 2.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"by":"Photo: Hoangkid (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"by":"Photo: Trúc Đồng (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🎋 Singing for tokens","q":"What does the word trù in ca trù mean?","a":"The woman in the middle, in a black áo dài, is the đào nương, the singer. Trù are bamboo tokens: in the old days, listeners bought tokens, and when the singing was good, they rewarded the đào nương with them; after the session, she exchanged the tokens for money. So ca trù means singing for tokens.","s":"Trù are bamboo tokens: listeners rewarded a fine singer with tokens, which she later exchanged for money."},{"tag":"🪵 The phách clappers","q":"What does the đào nương tap to keep time?","a":"The phách: two sticks beating on a small wooden block. The đào nương keeps her own time as she sings, and according to one study, students must master the phách even before training their voice. Mastering both voice and phách can take from three to ten years.","s":"The đào nương sings while tapping the phách to keep time, and students must learn the phách even before their voice."},{"tag":"🪕 The đàn đáy lute","q":"What's special about that long-necked lute?","a":"It's the đàn đáy: just three strings, a trapezoid-shaped body, and a very long neck with ten high frets. Đáy means bottom, yet its body has no bottom at all, which is why it was once also called vô để cầm, the bottomless lute. The player is called the kép, and always performs alongside the đào nương.","s":"The đàn đáy has three strings, a very long neck and high frets, and although đáy means bottom, its body has no bottom."},{"tag":"🥁 The praise drum","q":"What's the person by the small drum, holding a stick, doing?","a":"They're doing cầm chầu, keeping the praise drum. The drummer doesn't accompany the music, but uses the drum to praise or criticize, scoring each line. A stroke on the drumhead makes a tom sound; a stroke on the rim makes a chát. Two toms in a row are called song châu, three are liên châu, and each pattern carries its own message.","s":"The drummer doesn't accompany the music, but uses the praise drum to approve or criticize, scoring each line."},{"tag":"🎩 The quan viên","q":"In the old days, who got to hold this drumstick?","a":"Usually the quan viên: Confucian scholars and men of letters, people who understood both poetry and music. The poets Nguyễn Công Trứ and Dương Khuê once sat in that seat, and many quan viên were the very ones who wrote the words for the hát nói pieces the đào nương sang. In this session, though, the drumstick is in the hands of the woman in the pink áo dài."},{"tag":"🏛️ From village to city","q":"Where was ca trù sung in the old days?","a":"This session is on a wooden platform carved with lotuses, before a bamboo blind showing a pair of dragons. In the old days, ca trù was sung at the village communal house to honor the deities at festivals, and in private homes for an elder's longevity party or the Lunar New Year. In the early twentieth century, it moved into the singing houses of the cities, especially Hanoi, and that's when it was at its height."},{"tag":"🎼 56 styles","q":"How many ways of singing does ca trù have?","a":"UNESCO records fifty-six thể cách, or styles, each one a way of singing with its own musical framework. The lyrics use purely Vietnamese verse forms such as lục bát and song thất lục bát, and hát nói is considered the form best suited to ca trù of all.","card":{"big":"56 styles","sub":"Each style is a way of singing with its own musical framework"}},{"tag":"⚠️ Urgent safeguarding","q":"Why did ca trù have to go on the urgent safeguarding list?","a":"In the twentieth century, war and upheaval sent ca trù into decline; for a time, artists even had to hide away their lutes and clappers. Fewer and fewer people still knew how to sing, they were getting old, and learning takes enormous effort. In {2009|two thousand nine}, UNESCO placed ca trù on its urgent safeguarding list, as clubs were gradually bringing this way of singing back to life.","card":{"big":"2009","sub":"UNESCO: heritage in need of urgent safeguarding"}}]},"don-ca-tai-tu":{"title":"The Art of Đờn ca tài tử in Southern Vietnam","artist":"The community of artists and tài tử musicians of southern Vietnam (21 southern provinces and cities, according to the UNESCO file)","date":"Took shape in the late 19th century; inscribed by UNESCO on December 5, 2013","medium":"Instruments and singing: đàn kìm moon lute, đàn tranh zither, đàn cò fiddle, đàn bầu monochord, tiêu flute, song lang foot clapper, scalloped-fret guitar","where":"Throughout southern Vietnam, especially the Mekong Delta: in homes, at communal houses and temples, on death anniversaries, at weddings, in clubs","place":{"name":"Bạc Liêu, one cradle of đờn ca tài tử","prov":"Cà Mau"},"tags":["đờn ca tài tử","UNESCO","southern Vietnam","traditional instruments"],"hook":"In the old days, đờn ca tài tử players might spend the day plowing their fields; no one made a living from music. And yet, to earn a seat in a playing session, a musician had to study under a master for at least three years.","intro":"The art of đờn ca tài tử, music and song of southern Vietnam, took shape in the late nineteenth century and was inscribed by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in {2013|twenty thirteen}. This video uses no recordings and doesn't quote any lyrics.","openers":["Try to find the guitar in this session: in đờn ca tài tử, its fretboard is scooped out, and no, it isn't broken.","Stop and look at the instrument on the left with a body as round as the moon: together with the đàn tranh zither, it's the backbone of the whole session.","Did you notice that no one in the photo is reading sheet music? Each piece is only a framework; the rest is improvisation."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours listening to đờn ca tài tử…","end":"No stage, no printed score: just friends, a few instruments, the click of a song lang, and a framework for each player to embellish. Now you understand why people can sit and listen to đờn ca tài tử for hours without ever tiring of it.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Caitlin Childs (CC BY-SA 2.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0"},{"by":"Anonymous postcard, Saigon, 1911 (public domain)","lic":"Public domain"},{"by":"Photo: Bùi Thụy Đào Nguyên (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🎶 What tài tử means","q":"Why is this way of playing and singing called tài tử?","a":"According to Professor Trần Văn Khê, tài tử means people with talent who don't make music for a living. In the old days, they worked the fields by day and only gathered in the evening to play for one another. They played for love, but they were no dabblers: everyone had to study under a master for a very long time.","s":"Tài tử are talented people who don't live off their music: they farmed by day and gathered in the evening to play for one another."},{"tag":"🌙 The đàn kìm","q":"What's the instrument on the left, with a body as round as the moon?","a":"It's the đàn kìm, also called đàn nguyệt, the moon lute, which together with the đàn tranh zither forms the backbone of the session. Professor Trần Văn Khê described the plucked kìm as sounding steady, solemn and unhurried. His father, the master musician Trần Quang Triều, even invented his own way of tuning the kìm, which he named the Tố lan tuning."},{"tag":"🪕 Scalloped-fret guitar","q":"How is the guitar in this session different from an ordinary guitar?","a":"The wood between the frets is scooped out, which is why it's called a scalloped-fret guitar. That lets the fingers press deep into the strings, bending the pitch up and down, with the vibrato and ornaments that give tài tử music its character. According to some sources, from around the {1930s|nineteen thirties}, this Western instrument was scalloped and brought into tài tử and cải lương opera ensembles.","s":"The wood between the frets is scooped out so the fingers can press deep, bend the pitch and add the ornaments typical of tài tử music."},{"tag":"🏯 Roots in ca Huế","q":"Why does this Saigon ensemble from {1911|nineteen eleven} look so much like a ca Huế ensemble?","a":"Because that's where đờn ca tài tử comes from. In the late nineteenth century, court musicians of the Nguyễn dynasty who joined the Cần Vương movement went south, bringing the tradition of ca Huế, Huế's chamber singing, with them. Blending with ritual music and the folk songs of the river country, the repertoire gradually took on a southern voice. Some sources credit the court musician Nguyễn Quang Đại with a major role in this.","s":"Because that's its root: in the late nineteenth century, Nguyễn court musicians went south bringing ca Huế, and the repertoire gradually took on a southern voice."},{"tag":"🎻 Zither, lute, fiddle","q":"And the many-stringed instrument lying across the lap, and the long-necked one standing upright?","a":"That's the đàn tranh zither and the two-stringed đàn cò fiddle, which northerners call the đàn nhị. With the đàn kìm, they make up the classic trio of đờn ca tài tử. Each has its own technique: on the đàn tranh, the thumb often sweeps in one breath from the high strings down to the low ones, while the đàn cò uses lots of slides, its sound gliding softly along the string."},{"tag":"🦶 The song lang","q":"In a session, what keeps time for both the players and the singers?","a":"A small instrument played with the foot: the song lang. The player's foot pushes a little wooden ball on the end of a metal strip so that it strikes a round wooden body, making a crisp click. That click sounds on the key beats, and players and singers alike follow it to keep time.","card":{"big":"Song lang","sub":"A foot-played clapper that keeps time for the whole session"}},{"tag":"🎤 Frame and flourishes","q":"Do the singers have to hit every note exactly as written in a score?","a":"No. According to the UNESCO file, each piece has only a melodic framework and its main beats. Players and singers are free to embellish, adding flourishes to each phrase, as long as they meet on the right beat. That's why the same piece sounds a little different every time it's played.","s":"No: each piece is only a melodic framework, and players and singers embellish freely, as long as they meet on the beat."},{"tag":"📜 Twenty ancestral pieces","q":"To join a session, what does a musician have to know by heart?","a":"First of all, the twenty bài tổ, the ancestral pieces: six Bắc pieces, three Nam, four Oán and seven great pieces, as Professor Trần Văn Khê grouped them. The UNESCO file also mentions seventy-two classical pieces. A student has to follow a master for at least three years to get a grip on the playing technique and the basic modes.","card":{"big":"20 pieces","sub":"6 Bắc · 3 Nam · 4 Oán · 7 great pieces"}},{"tag":"🏅 UNESCO 2013","q":"When did UNESCO inscribe đờn ca tài tử?","a":"On {December 5, 2013|December fifth, twenty thirteen}, at a UNESCO session in Baku, Azerbaijan. The file says the art is practiced in twenty-one southern provinces and cities, and is still passed on mainly by listening to and imitating a master, from one generation to the next.","card":{"big":"2013","sub":"Inscribed by UNESCO in Baku, Azerbaijan"}}]},"bai-choi":{"title":"The Art of Bài Chòi in Central Vietnam","artist":"Bài chòi artists of central Vietnam: anh hiệu and chị hiệu callers, card makers, hut builders","date":"Centuries old (dating still disputed); inscribed by UNESCO on December 7, 2017","medium":"Card game and performance: riddle-calling, singing, gestures, drums, oboe, đàn nhị fiddle, clappers; huts of bamboo and leaves, printed picture cards","where":"Coastal villages of central Vietnam, from Quảng Bình to Khánh Hòa (9 provinces and cities within 2017 boundaries); bài chòi festivals at Tết, bài chòi on a mat, bài chòi folk theater","place":{"name":"Hoài Nhơn, regarded as the ancestral home of bài chòi (formerly Bình Định)","prov":"Gia Lai"},"tags":["bài chòi","UNESCO","Lunar New Year","central Vietnam"],"hook":"At Tết, the Lunar New Year, the whole village builds bamboo huts out in the yard, then climbs in to wait. They're holding their breath not over money, but because someone is about to sing a riddle.","intro":"The art of bài chòi in central Vietnam, both a card game and a folk performance of the region's villages, inscribed by UNESCO as Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in {2017|twenty seventeen}. This video doesn't quote any of the calls or songs; it just tells the story behind them.","openers":["Why would anyone build a whole row of bamboo huts just to play a game of cards?","Stop and look at the hut on the right: sitting inside, the players wait for each sung riddle from the anh hiệu, the caller.","Try to find the red flag on top of the hut: at a bài chòi festival, flags are also prizes."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours in a bamboo hut, just listening to bài chòi…","end":"A card game where winning and losing hardly matter: all it takes is a good song, the knock of a wooden block, and a little luck for the new year. Now you understand why people in central Vietnam can sit in bamboo huts listening to bài chòi all through Tết.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Lưu Ly (CC BY 3.0)","lic":"CC BY 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: LÊ TẤN LỘC (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Lưu Ly (CC BY 3.0)","lic":"CC BY 3.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🛖 Bamboo huts","q":"Why do the card players have to sit up in bamboo huts?","a":"The huts are built of bamboo, with floors raised off the ground. According to researcher Đặng Hoành Loan, the floor used to be about one and a half meters high, level with the caller's forehead: just the right height to hand cards or prize money straight to the players. Many also believe the game began in the lookout huts of hillside fields, where the people keeping watch called out to one another to chase off wild animals.","s":"The hut floors were once level with the caller's forehead, just right for handing cards to the players, and many believe the game began in field lookout huts."},{"tag":"🏮 Main hut, player huts","q":"How many people sit in each hut like this one?","a":"About three to five. A festival usually puts up nine or eleven huts, arranged in a U shape: one main hut for the organizers, and the rest are player huts. Some places even name the huts after the twelve animals of the zodiac, or after signs from the I Ching, the Book of Changes."},{"tag":"🎙️ The anh hiệu","q":"What role does the man in blue, holding a microphone, play?","a":"That's the anh hiệu, the caller, the soul of the game. According to researcher Đặng Hoành Loan, the anh hiệu alone creates the atmosphere of the whole festival: singing, improvising lyrics, acting them out with gestures, and tapping the sênh clappers to keep time. Quick wits and humor are a good anh hiệu's stock in trade.","s":"That's the anh hiệu, the soul of the game: singing, improvising, acting it out and tapping the sênh clappers to keep time."},{"tag":"🎋 The riddle call","q":"When a card is drawn from that red tube, why isn't its name read out right away?","a":"Because first comes the hô thai, the riddle call. That tube is the card tube, holding the individual cards. After drawing one, the caller sings a riddle-like verse, and only its very last word reveals the card's name. The people in the huts listen and guess, and the whole yard holds its breath for that final word.","s":"Because first comes the riddle call: a sung verse like a riddle, and only its last word reveals the card's name."},{"tag":"🃏 Cards on bamboo","q":"What are those bamboo sticks in the old woman's hand?","a":"They're the cards. Today the cards are usually printed on paper and glued onto bamboo sticks, flat at one end and small and round at the other. Each card has a picture, in some places with Chinese characters, and the names are wonderfully down-to-earth: Nhứt Nọc, Nhì Nghèo, Ba Gà, Tứ Tượng, Bảy Liễu, Ông Ầm. The number of cards and the pictures vary from region to region."},{"tag":"🔔 Knock for a match","q":"Sitting in a hut, how do the players announce a match?","a":"By knocking a mõ, a wooden block. Depending on the region, each hut gets one bamboo stick printed with three cards, or picks five sticks itself. When the anh hiệu calls the card a hut is waiting for, its players knock the mõ. Get enough cards under the rules and you're tới: you win the round, though the rules for winning vary by region."},{"tag":"🚩 Prize flags","q":"What does the red flag on top of the hut have to do with the game?","a":"It may be good news. At many festivals, when a hut wins a round, the anh hiệu brings it a prize flag to hang on its roof, amid drums, oboes, the đàn nhị fiddle and clappers. Each flag stands for only a small stake: at the end of the festival, one flag means you broke even, and two or more mean good luck for the new year.","s":"It may be good news: a hut that wins a round gets a prize flag on its roof, and the prize is small, because people play for new-year luck."},{"tag":"🎭 Onto the stage","q":"How did bài chòi go from a Tết game to the stage?","a":"Beyond the festivals, anh hiệu performers also spread out mats and performed from village to village, which is called bài chòi chiếu, bài chòi on a mat. According to Văn hóa newspaper, in mid-{1933|nineteen thirty-three}, bài chòi was put on a wooden stage for the first time, at An Lương market in Phù Mỹ, Bình Định, paving the way for professional bài chòi folk theater later on.","card":{"big":"1933","sub":"Bài chòi's first wooden stage, in Bình Định"}},{"tag":"🏅 UNESCO 2017","q":"When did UNESCO inscribe bài chòi?","a":"On {December 7, 2017|December seventh, twenty seventeen}, on Jeju Island, South Korea. That year's file covered nine coastal provinces and cities of central Vietnam, from Quảng Bình to Khánh Hòa. Artists and communities have set up nearly ninety troupes, groups and clubs to pass the art on to young people.","card":{"big":"2017","sub":"Inscribed by UNESCO on Jeju Island, South Korea"}}]},"gom-cham":{"title":"The Art of Chăm Pottery-Making","artist":"Chăm women of Bàu Trúc and Bình Đức villages (the practicing community)","date":"A traditional craft, said in legend to date from the Champa era (around the 12th century); inscribed by UNESCO on November 29, 2022","medium":"Clay mixed with sand, shaped by hand with no wheel, no molds and no glaze; fired in the open with wood, straw and rice husks","where":"Bàu Trúc village (Ninh Phước commune, Khánh Hòa province; formerly Ninh Thuận) and Bình Đức village (Phan Hiệp hamlet, Bắc Bình commune, Lâm Đồng province; formerly Bình Thuận)","place":{"name":"Bàu Trúc pottery village","prov":"Khánh Hòa"},"tags":["Chăm pottery","Bàu Trúc","UNESCO","handicraft"],"hook":"Of these hundreds of pieces of pottery, not one ever touched a potter's wheel. While the clay is shaped, it stays still, and it's the potter who walks around it.","intro":"The art of pottery-making of the Chăm people, in the villages of Bàu Trúc and Bình Đức, inscribed by UNESCO on the List of Intangible Cultural Heritage in Need of Urgent Safeguarding in {2022|twenty twenty-two}. The photos show pottery from Bàu Trúc village.","openers":["Try to find the potter's wheel that made these jars. You won't: Chăm potters walk around the clay themselves.","Stop and look at the rope wrapped around the neck of the big jar on the left: it's made of clay too.","Here's a detail among the jars: clay spheres pierced with stars and crescent moons."],"trendLine":"Now I finally understand why people can spend hours looking at a single clay jar…","end":"No wheel, no kiln, no glaze: just two hands, footsteps walking backwards, and a fire under the open sky. Now you understand why people can gaze at Chăm pottery for hours without ever tiring of it.","img":[{"by":"Photo: Liftold (CC BY-SA 3.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"by":"Photo: Windrain (CC BY-SA 4.0)","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"steps":[{"tag":"🔄 No potter's wheel","q":"Were these jars made on a potter's wheel?","a":"There's no wheel at all. During shaping, the clay sits still on a stand, and the potter walks backwards around it, her hands pressing and smoothing as she goes. Villagers joke that it's made by hand and spun by the backside. That's why the jars look alike, yet no two are exactly the same.","s":"No wheel: the clay sits still on a stand while the potter walks backwards around it, so no two jars are exactly alike."},{"tag":"🏞️ Quao River clay","q":"Where does the clay for these jars come from?","a":"From a field near the Quao River, a few kilometers from Bàu Trúc village. The dark gray, very pliable clay is soaked for about twelve hours, then mixed with sand, on average one part clay to one part sand, and kneaded first with the feet, then by hand. Knead it carelessly, and the pottery will crack in the fire."},{"tag":"🧣 Wet cloth, bamboo rings","q":"Without a wheel, how do the jar necks come out so round and smooth?","a":"Thanks to surprisingly simple tools. A piece of coarse cloth dipped in water to smooth the rim and body until they're evenly round, a few bamboo rings to scrape the walls smooth, plus clam shells and seashells. Some places even rub the pot with a polishing stone before it's fired."},{"tag":"🐚 Seashell patterns","q":"What are the ropes and zigzag lines on these jars made with?","a":"With things found right around the house: the edge of a seashell, a flower bud, a bamboo stick, a broken comb, pressed into the still-wet clay. Familiar patterns include zigzags, waves, leaves, and ropes of clay added in relief around the neck. There are no ready-made molds, so every piece is one of a kind.","s":"With seashells, flower buds, bamboo sticks and comb teeth pressed into the wet clay; with no molds, every piece is one of a kind."},{"tag":"🏮 Clay lamps","q":"What are those spheres pierced with stars and crescent moons?","a":"They're clay lamps. In the past, Chăm pottery was mostly for cooking and holding water: pots, stoves, jars and urns. Today the village also makes lamps, vases, statues of deities and of Apsara dancers, and figures of people and animals in the style of ancient Champa art appear on the pottery more and more."},{"tag":"🔥 Fired in the open","q":"Why are the jars and vases in front of the cooperative orange-red?","a":"Because of the fire. Chăm pottery isn't fired in a kiln or glazed: the raw pieces are stacked on a bed of firewood out in an open field, covered with straw and rice husks, and set alight. According to the UNESCO file, the fire burns for about seven to eight hours, at around eight hundred degrees. In the flames, the gray clay turns a pinkish red, sometimes with brown or black patches, depending on the wind.","s":"The fire: no kiln, no glaze; covered in wood, straw and husks, the raw pots burn outdoors for seven to eight hours, and the gray clay turns pinkish red."},{"tag":"👩‍👧 Mother to daughter","q":"Who has kept Bàu Trúc's pottery craft alive through the generations?","a":"Chăm women. Shaping pottery was women's work, handed down the mother's line, or as villagers say, mẹ truyền, con nối: mother passes on, daughter carries on. Girls of twelve or thirteen sat watching their mothers, then tried it themselves. Master potter Trương Thị Gạch says she learned from her mother, who had learned from her own mother.","s":"Chăm women: the craft passed from mother to daughter, or as villagers say, mẹ truyền, con nối: mother passes on, daughter carries on."},{"tag":"🔁 Five laps, then reverse","q":"In the second Chăm pottery village in the UNESCO file, how does the potter walk?","a":"In Bình Đức village, the potter bends slightly forward and walks counterclockwise around the stand for about five laps, then changes direction. The number of laps depends on the shape of the piece, while the walls keep rising from the coils of clay added along the way.","card":{"big":"5 laps","sub":"Bình Đức village: five laps, then change direction"}},{"tag":"🆘 Urgent safeguarding","q":"Why does Chăm pottery need urgent safeguarding?","a":"On {November 29, 2022|November twenty-ninth, twenty twenty-two}, in Rabat, Morocco, UNESCO inscribed the pottery-making of the Chăm people in the two villages of Bàu Trúc and Bình Đức on its urgent safeguarding list. Urbanization is making clay harder to come by, the master potters are getting older, and few young people want to take up the craft.","card":{"big":"2022","sub":"UNESCO: heritage in need of urgent safeguarding"}}]}}};
